OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026", "title": "Algebraic Kuga–Satake correspondences and Hodge conjectures on a K3 quadratic locus", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "032", "family_title": "The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties", "abstract_html": "We prove the rational Hodge and generalized Hodge conjectures in every cohomological degree of every self-power of a projective complex K3 surface whose transcendental quadratic space, with its cup-product form, admits a rational isometric embedding in \\(\\mathbb U_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\oplus2}\\perp\\langle-1\\rangle^4\\). This includes every ample <i>P</i>-polarized K3 surface, including all Picard jumps, for \\(P=\\mathbb U\\oplus D_8(-1)\\oplus D_4(-1)\\). On this locus we construct algebraic correspondences inducing every prescribed standard even-Clifford Kuga–Satake tensor. More generally, for any projective complex K3 surface, algebraicity of one exact standard even-Clifford Kuga–Satake tensor implies both conjectures for every self-power.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If, for one such \\(w\\), one rational polarization, and one full even-Clifford realization, the exact tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) is induced by a cycle in \\( \\operatorname{CH}^{2}(S\\times A^{2})_{\\mathbf Q}, \\) then rational …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All cohomology groups have rational coefficients unless an integral lattice is specified. All varieties are over \\(\\mathbf C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth projective variety \\(X\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Hdg}^{r}(X)=H^{2r}(X,\\mathbf Q)\\cap H^{r,r}(X),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  N^{p}H^{j}(X,\\mathbf Q)\n  =\n  \\sum_{\\substack{B\\subset X\\ \\mathrm{closed}\\\\ \\operatorname{codim}_{X}B\\ge p}}\n  \\ker\\!\\left(H^{j}(X,\\mathbf Q)\\longrightarrow H^{j}(X\\setminus B,\\mathbf Q)\\right).\n  \\]\n  Rational HC means that every rational Hodge class is algebraic. Rational GHC means that \\(N^{p}H^{j}(X,\\mathbf Q)\\) is the largest rational sub-Hodge structure whose types \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a,b\\ge p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a projective K3 surface, and let\n  \\[\n  T_S=\\operatorname{NS}(S)_{\\mathbf Q}^{\\perp}\\subset H^{2}(S,\\mathbf Q)\n  \\]\n  with cup-product form \\(q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Clifford convention \\(v^{2}=q(v,v)\\). Let\n  \\[\n  W=C^{+}(T_S,q)=H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\n  \\]\n  be a specified full even-Clifford Kuga–Satake realization. Its complex structure is left multiplication by \\(J=e_1e_2\\), where \\(e_1,e_2\\) are an oriented orthonormal basis of the positive period plane.</p><p class=\"li\">For a rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T_S\\), define\n  \\[\n  j_w:T_S(1)\\longrightarrow \\operatorname{End}(W),\n  \\qquad j_w(v)(a)=vaw.\n  \\]\n  For a rational polarization \\(E\\) of \\(W\\), let\n  \\[\n  E^\\flat:W\\longrightarrow W^\\vee(-1),\\qquad y\\longmapsto E(y,-).\n  \\]\n  Converting the second factor of \\(\\operatorname{End}(W)=W\\otimes W^\\vee\\) by \\((E^\\flat)^{-1}\\), with the corresponding Tate twist, gives\n  \\[\n  \\iota_{w,E}:T_S\\longrightarrow W\\otimes W\n  \\subset H^{2}(A\\times A,\\mathbf Q).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If, for one such \\(w\\), one rational polarization, and one full even-Clifford realization, the exact tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) is induced by a cycle in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CH}^{2}(S\\times A^{2})_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\]\nthen rational HC and rational GHC hold in every cohomological degree of every self-power of \\(S\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The exact tensor hypothesis gives the exterior-power returns of Proposition \\(2\\). These algebraize the relative determinant tensors and, together with endomorphism-field actions and invariant theory, give ordinary HC by Proposition \\(4\\). Their geometric support property in Proposition \\(3\\), the exterior carriers of Proposition \\(5\\), and the correspondence assembly of Proposition \\(6\\) give GHC in every degree of every power. This proves the theorem independently of the concrete abelian-cover construction.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For any specified full even-Clifford realization \\( H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)=C^{+}(T_S,q), \\) any rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T_S\\), and any rational polarization \\(E\\), there exists …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb U\\) be the hyperbolic plane with Gram matrix\n  \\[\n  \\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n  \\]\n  Put\n  \\[\n  P=\\mathbb U\\oplus D_8(-1)\\oplus D_4(-1),\n  \\qquad\n  V_P=(\\mathbb U\\oplus\\mathbb U(2)\\oplus D_4(-1))_{\\mathbf Q}\n  \\simeq \\mathbb U_{\\mathbf Q}^{\\oplus2}\\perp\\langle-1\\rangle^{4}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An ample \\(P\\)-polarization is a primitive embedding \\(P\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{NS}(S)\\) whose image contains an ample class.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a smooth projective complex K3 surface admitting a rational isometric embedding\n  \\[\n  (T_S,q)\\hookrightarrow V_P.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The exact standard tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) is defined as in Theorem \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For any specified full even-Clifford realization\n\\[\nH^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)=C^{+}(T_S,q),\n\\]\nany rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T_S\\), and any rational polarization \\(E\\), there exists\n\\[\n\\Gamma_S\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{2}(S\\times A\\times A)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n(\\Gamma_S)_*x=\\iota_{w,E}(x)\n\\qquad(x\\in T_S)\n\\]\nin the full group \\(H^{2}(A\\times A,\\mathbf Q)\\). The same assertion holds on a transported isogenous model with its specified tensor.</p><p>Moreover, rational HC and rational GHC hold in every cohomological degree of \\(S^{m}\\), for every integer \\(m\\ge1\\).</p><p>In particular, these assertions apply to every ample \\(P\\)-polarized K3 surface, including all Picard jumps.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(21\\) proves algebraicity of every stipulated exact standard tensor under the rational isometric-embedding hypothesis. Choose one such tensor and apply Theorem \\(1\\). Rational HC and rational GHC follow in every degree of every self-power.</p><p>For an ample \\(P\\)-polarized surface, \\(T_S\\) is a nondegenerate subspace of the fixed perpendicular\n\\[\nU_P\\simeq V_P\n\\]\nby Proposition \\(1\\). Thus all such surfaces, including Picard jumps, satisfy the hypothesis.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary \\(9.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(n,r\\ge1\\), rational HC and rational GHC hold in every degree of \\( (S^{[n]})^{r}. \\) More generally, let \\(\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{Br}(S)\\), and let \\(M\\) be either:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a smooth projective complex K3 surface admitting a rational isometric embedding\n  \\[\n  (T_S,q)\\hookrightarrow V_P,\n  \\]\n  with \\(V_P\\) as in Theorem \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S^{[n]}\\) denote the Hilbert scheme of length-\\(n\\) subschemes of \\(S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(n,r\\ge1\\), rational HC and rational GHC hold in every degree of\n\\[\n(S^{[n]})^{r}.\n\\]</p><p>More generally, let \\(\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{Br}(S)\\), and let \\(M\\) be either:</p><p class=\"li\">a smooth projective moduli space of Gieseker-stable \\(\\alpha\\)-twisted sheaves on \\(S\\); or</p><p class=\"li\">a smooth projective moduli space of \\(\\sigma\\)-stable objects in \\(D^{b}(S,\\alpha)\\), for a generic Bridgeland stability condition \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p>Then rational HC and rational GHC hold in every degree of \\(M^{r}\\), for every \\(r\\ge1\\). If \\(M\\) is hyperkähler, the same conclusions hold for every smooth projective hyperkähler variety birational to \\(M\\), and for all its self-powers.</p>", "proof": "<p>The case of zero-dimensional \\(M\\) is immediate. Write \\(h(-)\\) for the rational Chow motive.</p><p>The de Cataldo–Migliorini decomposition [10, Theorem \\(6.2.1\\)] expresses \\(h(S^{[n]})\\) as a sum of Tate twists of summands of motives of powers of \\(S\\); rational averaging projectors cut out the symmetric-product summands. For either stated moduli space, Bülles [8, Theorem \\(0.1\\)] makes \\(h(M)\\) a direct summand of a finite sum of Tate twists of \\(h(S^k)\\). Tensoring the split correspondences gives analogous splittings for every self-power.</p><p>These varieties are therefore motivated by \\(S\\) in Arapura’s sense. Theorem \\(2\\) gives HC and GHC for all powers of \\(S\\); Arapura’s transfer lemma [2, Lemma \\(4.2\\)], retaining geometric coniveau with its Tate shifts, gives both conjectures for all stated powers.</p><p>Finally, Rieß’s specialization correspondence for birational smooth projective hyperkähler varieties gives an isomorphism of Chow motives, as cited in [32, Theorem \\(3.2\\) and Section \\(3.3\\)] and formulated in [8, Proposition \\(1.4\\)]. The same transfer proves the birational assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma \\(1.3\\), together with the following existence observation", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a primitive embedding \\(P\\hookrightarrow\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\) with orthogonal complement \\( T_P=\\mathbb U\\oplus\\mathbb U(2)\\oplus D_4(-1). \\) For every primitive embedding of \\(P\\) in the K3 lattice, its …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}=\\mathbb U^{\\oplus3}\\oplus E_8(-1)^{\\oplus2},\n  \\qquad\n  P=\\mathbb U\\oplus D_8(-1)\\oplus D_4(-1).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a primitive embedding \\(P\\hookrightarrow\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\) with orthogonal complement\n\\[\nT_P=\\mathbb U\\oplus\\mathbb U(2)\\oplus D_4(-1).\n\\]\nFor every primitive embedding of \\(P\\) in the K3 lattice, its rational orthogonal complement is isometric to \\(V_P\\).</p><p>There exists a projective K3 surface with Néron–Severi lattice exactly \\(P\\); its \\(P\\)-polarization can be chosen ample.</p>", "proof": "<p>For an even lattice \\(L\\), write \\(A_L=L^{*}/L\\) and \\(q_L:A_L\\to\\mathbf Q/2\\mathbf Z\\). The discriminant forms of \\(D_8(-1)\\) and \\(D_4(-1)\\) are respectively the split and anisotropic forms \\(u,v\\) on \\((\\mathbf Z/2)^2\\). Their values are integral modulo \\(2\\mathbf Z\\), so each is isomorphic to its negative. Also \\(q_{\\mathbb U(2)}=u\\). Hence\n\\[\n(A_P,q_P)\\simeq u\\oplus v\\simeq(A_{T_P},-q_{T_P}).\n\\]\nAn anti-isometry glues \\(P\\oplus T_P\\) to an even unimodular lattice of signature \\((3,19)\\), hence to \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\), with both summands primitive.</p><p>For another primitive embedding, Witt’s extension theorem applied to the two copies of \\(P_{\\mathbf Q}\\) gives an isometry of \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3},\\mathbf Q}\\) carrying one copy to the other and therefore identifying their rational perpendiculars. Rescaling one isotropic basis vector identifies \\(\\mathbb U(2)_{\\mathbf Q}\\) with \\(\\mathbb U_{\\mathbf Q}\\), and the coordinate description of \\(D_4\\) gives\n\\[\nD_4(-1)_{\\mathbf Q}\\simeq\\langle-1\\rangle^4.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a period in the domain of \\(T_P\\) outside the countably many hyperplanes defined by integral vectors not in \\(P\\). Period surjectivity gives a K3 surface with Néron–Severi group exactly \\(P\\). It is projective because \\(P\\) contains a positive class, and its ample cone contains a primitive integral class of \\(P\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(1\\le k\\le t\\), alternated composition of \\(k\\) copies of \\(j_w\\) gives an injective algebraic Hodge map \\( F_k:\\bigwedge^{k}V\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}(W) \\) with an algebraic left inverse on …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a projective K3 surface, put\n  \\[\n  N=\\operatorname{NS}(S)_{\\mathbf Q},\\qquad T=N^\\perp,\\qquad V=T(1),\n  \\qquad t=\\dim_{\\mathbf Q}T.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W=C^{+}(T,q)=H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\), and suppose the tensor form of\n  \\[\n  j_w(v)(a)=vaw\n  \\]\n  is induced by a cycle in \\(\\operatorname{CH}^{2}(S\\times A^{2})_{\\mathbf Q}\\), for one rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T\\) and one rational polarization.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(Z=A^{2}\\) and \\(n=\\dim Z\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(1\\le k\\le t\\), alternated composition of \\(k\\) copies of \\(j_w\\) gives an injective algebraic Hodge map\n\\[\nF_k:\\bigwedge^{k}V\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}(W)\n\\]\nwith an algebraic left inverse on cohomology. More precisely, there are correspondences\n\\[\n\\Gamma_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{k+1}(S^{k}\\times Z)_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\qquad\nR_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{n+k-1}(Z\\times S^{k})_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nwhose weight-zero actions satisfy\n\\[\n(\\Gamma_k)_*|_{\\wedge^{k}V}=F_k,\\qquad (R_k)_*F_k=\\operatorname{id}.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\operatorname{End}(W)\\simeq\n\\bigl(H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\otimes H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\bigr)(1)\n\\subset H^{2}(Z,\\mathbf Q)(1)\n\\]\nis identified using a rational polarization.</p><p>Every rational Hodge class in \\(\\bigwedge^{k}V\\) is the class of a rational algebraic cycle of codimension \\(k\\) on \\(S^{k}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(2.1\\)–\\(2.3\\) construct the asserted maps and left inverses. The composite \\(\\Gamma_kR_k\\) is the cohomological projector onto \\(F_k(\\bigwedge^{k}T)\\subset H^{2}(Z,\\mathbf Q)\\).</p><p>If \\(\\alpha\\in(\\bigwedge^{k}V)_{\\mathbf Q}^{0,0}\\), then \\(F_k(\\alpha)\\) is a rational \\((1,1)\\)-class in \\(B_W\\). Lefschetz’s \\((1,1)\\)-theorem represents it by a rational divisor on \\(Z\\). Applying \\(R_k\\) returns \\(\\alpha\\), represented by a cycle of codimension\n\\[\n1+(n+k-1)-n=k\n\\]\non \\(S^{k}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The cycles \\( \\pi_0=[o]\\times S,\\quad \\pi_4=S\\times[o],\\quad \\pi_N=\\sum_{i,j}b_{ij}D_i\\times D_j,\\quad \\pi_T=\\Delta_S-\\pi_0-\\pi_4-\\pi_N \\) are the indicated cohomological projectors.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the surface \\(S\\), and the spaces \\(N,T\\), of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a point \\(o\\in S\\), a divisor basis \\(D_1,\\ldots,D_\\rho\\) of \\(N\\), and let \\((b_{ij})\\) be the inverse of \\((D_i\\cdot D_j)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cycles\n\\[\n\\pi_0=[o]\\times S,\\quad\n\\pi_4=S\\times[o],\\quad\n\\pi_N=\\sum_{i,j}b_{ij}D_i\\times D_j,\\quad\n\\pi_T=\\Delta_S-\\pi_0-\\pi_4-\\pi_N\n\\]\nare the indicated cohomological projectors. The pairing and inverse pairing on \\(T\\) are algebraic.</p><p>The product of \\(k\\) copies of \\(\\pi_T\\), followed by\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Alt}_k=\\frac1{k!}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\mathfrak S_k}\n\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\sigma,\n\\]\nis an algebraic self-adjoint cohomological projector \\(\\pi_{\\wedge,k}\\) on \\(S^{k}\\), with image\n\\[\n\\bigwedge^{k}T\\subset H^{2k}(S^{k},\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nIts middle intersection pairing is nondegenerate.</p><p>Algebraic degree-one projectors on abelian varieties are available. Algebraicity with coefficients in a field \\(K\\supset\\mathbf Q\\) means membership in the scalar extension of the rational cycle-class image, and rational classes descend:\n\\[\nH_{\\mathbf Q}\\cap(A_{\\mathbf Q}\\otimes_{\\mathbf Q}K)=A_{\\mathbf Q}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The intersection form on \\(N\\) is nondegenerate by Hodge index. The displayed projectors act as stated. The pairing on \\(T\\) is intersection, and its inverse pairing is the transcendental component of the diagonal.</p><p>All transcendental factors have even degree, so permutations act by ordinary permutations of tensor factors. Transposition replaces a permutation by its inverse; hence \\(\\pi_{\\wedge,k}\\) is self-adjoint. Its induced pairing is a nonzero rational multiple of the exterior pairing associated with \\(q\\), and is therefore nondegenerate.</p><p>If \\(\\dim A=g\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\) is an integer, the polynomial\n\\[\n\\pi_1^A=\n\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le j\\le2g\\\\j\\ne1}}\n\\frac{[a]^*-a^{j}\\operatorname{id}}{a-a^{j}}\n\\]\nin the transpose graph of multiplication by \\(a\\) projects onto \\(H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\).</p><p>For coefficient descent, tensoring \\(H_{\\mathbf Q}/A_{\\mathbf Q}\\) with a field is faithful. Products, permutations, and contractions by algebraic pairings preserve this notion of algebraicity. Only coefficients, not the varieties over \\(\\mathbf C\\), are extended.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Alternated composition satisfies \\( F_k(\\alpha)(1)=c_k(\\alpha)w^{k}. \\) It is injective for every \\(1\\le k\\le t\\), is a Hodge map, and has an algebraic tensor realization …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the exact Clifford-map hypothesis of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c_k:\\bigwedge^{k}T\\to C(T,q)\\) be antisymmetrized Clifford multiplication.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Alternated composition satisfies\n\\[\nF_k(\\alpha)(1)=c_k(\\alpha)w^{k}.\n\\]\nIt is injective for every \\(1\\le k\\le t\\), is a Hodge map, and has an algebraic tensor realization\n\\[\n\\Gamma_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{k+1}(S^{k}\\times Z)_{\\mathbf Q}.\n\\]\nIn untwisted notation its action is\n\\[\nF_k:\\bigwedge^{k}T\\longrightarrow B_W(1-k),\n\\qquad\nB_W=H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\otimes H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\subset H^{2}(Z,\\mathbf Q).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Associativity gives\n\\[\nj_w(v_1)\\cdots j_w(v_k)(a)=v_1\\cdots v_k\\,a\\,w^{k}.\n\\]\nThus evaluation at \\(a=1\\) gives the asserted formula. Clifford PBW identifies \\(c_k\\) with the degree-\\(k\\) exterior summand, so \\(c_k\\) is injective. Since\n\\[\nw^{-1}=\\frac{w}{q(w,w)},\n\\]\nright multiplication by \\(w^{k}\\) is invertible. For odd \\(k\\), both left and right multiplications are odd and their composite preserves \\(C^{+}(T,q)\\).</p><p>The map \\(j_w\\) is Hodge for the left spin action; tensor products, composition, and alternation preserve this property.</p><p>Choose an ample class \\(\\ell\\) on \\(A\\), and use\n\\[\ne(x,y)=\\int_A x\\cup y\\cup\\ell^{g-1}.\n\\]\nChanging the rational polarization preserves algebraicity, because the change on the identified dual factor is a rational Hodge automorphism of \\(H^{1}(A,\\mathbf Q)\\), hence comes from an abelian homomorphism with rational coefficients.</p><p>Project the given cycle by \\(\\pi_T\\) and \\(\\pi_1^A\\boxtimes\\pi_1^A\\), take its \\(k\\)-fold exterior product, and project the input by \\(\\pi_{\\wedge,k}\\). Composition in \\(\\operatorname{End}(W)\\) contracts \\(k-1\\) pairs of \\(W\\)-slots by \\(e\\). Each contraction is algebraic, represented by\n\\[\n(\\Delta_A)_*(\\ell^{g-1})\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{2g-1}(A^{2})_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nas a correspondence to a point. Each lowers cohomological degree by two. Signs from permuting degree-one factors are included in the coefficients. The result has degree shift \\(2-2k\\), hence codimension \\(k+1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an algebraic map \\( G_k:H^{2}(Z,\\mathbf Q)\\longrightarrow\\bigwedge^{k}T, \\qquad G_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{n+k-1}(Z\\times S^{k})_{\\mathbf Q}, \\) such that \\(D_k=G_kF_k\\) is invertible on \\(\\bigwedge^{k}T\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(F_k,\\Gamma_k,B_W,Z\\) of Lemma \\(2.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  L=\\operatorname{pr}_1^*\\ell+\\operatorname{pr}_2^*\\ell.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an algebraic map\n\\[\nG_k:H^{2}(Z,\\mathbf Q)\\longrightarrow\\bigwedge^{k}T,\n\\qquad\nG_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{n+k-1}(Z\\times S^{k})_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\]\nsuch that \\(D_k=G_kF_k\\) is invertible on \\(\\bigwedge^{k}T\\). A polynomial inverse produces\n\\[\nR_k=P_k(D_k)G_k,\\qquad R_kF_k=\\operatorname{id}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The summand \\(B_W\\) is primitive in degree two for \\(L\\). Every term of \\(L^{n-1}B_W\\) has odd degree in each abelian factor, while the only possible top bidegree is \\((2g,2g)\\). Thus \\(L^{n-1}B_W=0\\).</p><p>Up to a fixed sign, Hodge–Riemann makes\n\\[\nQ_Z(x,y)=\\int_Z x\\cup y\\cup L^{n-2}\n\\]\na polarization on \\(B_W\\). The image of\n\\[\n(\\bigwedge^{k}T)(k-1)\\longrightarrow B_W\n\\]\nis a Hodge substructure, so \\(Q_Z\\) restricts nondegenerately to it.</p><p>Multiplication by \\(L^{n-2}\\), followed by \\({}^{t}\\Gamma_k\\) and \\(\\pi_{\\wedge,k}\\), gives \\(G_k\\). If \\(Q_k\\) is middle intersection on \\(\\bigwedge^{k}T\\), self-adjointness gives\n\\[\nQ_k(G_kF_kx,y)=Q_Z(F_kx,F_ky).\n\\]\nBoth pairings are nondegenerate, so \\(D_k\\) is invertible.</p><p>Its characteristic polynomial has nonzero constant term. Cayley–Hamilton gives \\(P_k\\in\\mathbf Q[X]\\) with \\(P_k(D_k)D_k=\\operatorname{id}\\). Composition of cycles realizes this polynomial using \\(\\pi_{\\wedge,k}\\) as the identity of the summand. Therefore\n\\[\nR_k=P_k(D_k)G_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{n+k-1}(Z\\times S^{k})_{\\mathbf Q}.\n\\]\nNo algebraic inverse Lefschetz operator is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(1\\le k\\le t\\), \\( \\bigwedge^{k}T\\subset N^{k-1}H^{2k}(S^{k},\\mathbf Q). \\) Every rational sub-Hodge structure of this exterior power satisfies GHC in its ambient \\(H^{2k}(S^{k},\\mathbf Q)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) satisfy the exact even-Clifford algebraicity hypothesis of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(T=T_S\\) and \\(t=\\dim_{\\mathbf Q}T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(N^{p}H^{j}(X,\\mathbf Q)=H^{j}(X,\\mathbf Q)\\) for \\(p\\le0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(1\\le k\\le t\\),\n\\[\n\\bigwedge^{k}T\\subset N^{k-1}H^{2k}(S^{k},\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nEvery rational sub-Hodge structure of this exterior power satisfies GHC in its ambient \\(H^{2k}(S^{k},\\mathbf Q)\\). In particular, \\(\\bigwedge^{t}T\\) is supported in codimension \\(t\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\nR_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{n+k-1}(Z\\times S^{k})_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\qquad\nR_kF_k=\\operatorname{id}.\n\\]\nLemma \\(3.1\\) puts its image from \\(H^{2}(Z,\\mathbf Q)\\) in coniveau \\(k-1\\); this image contains \\(\\bigwedge^{k}T\\).</p><p>The only possible types of \\(\\bigwedge^{k}T\\) are\n\\[\n(k+1,k-1),\\quad(k,k),\\quad(k-1,k+1),\n\\]\nbecause \\(T^{2,0}\\) and \\(T^{0,2}\\) are one-dimensional. A sub-Hodge structure requiring coniveau greater than \\(k-1\\) is therefore of type \\((k,k)\\). Proposition \\(2\\) algebraizes every rational vector in it. A finite rational basis and the union of the supports of its codimension-\\(k\\) cycles give coniveau \\(k\\). This includes the top exterior line, whose only type is \\((t,t)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a fixed closed subset \\(B\\subset Y\\) of codimension at least \\(p\\) supporting the image of \\(C_*\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X,Y\\) be smooth projective varieties, with \\(\\dim X=N\\), \\(\\dim Y=D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge0\\), and let\n  \\[\n  C\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{N+p}(X\\times Y)_{\\mathbf Q}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed closed subset \\(B\\subset Y\\) of codimension at least \\(p\\) supporting the image of \\(C_*\\). In particular,\n\\[\nC_*H^{j}(X,\\mathbf Q)\\subset N^{p}H^{j+2p}(Y,\\mathbf Q).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a finite cycle representative for \\(C\\). Each component has dimension \\(D-p\\), so the union \\(B\\) of its proper images in \\(Y\\) has dimension at most \\(D-p\\). The correspondence restricts to zero on \\(X\\times(Y\\setminus B)\\). Compatibility of its action with restriction proves the assertion, also for rational linear combinations.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Sections \\(2.4\\)–\\(2.5\\), the ordinary Hodge assertion of Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every rational Hodge tensor in \\(V^{\\otimes r}\\) is algebraic as a codimension-\\(r\\) class on \\(S^{r}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) satisfy the exact even-Clifford algebraicity hypothesis of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(T=T_S\\), \\(V=T(1)\\), and \\(E=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(T)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every rational Hodge tensor in \\(V^{\\otimes r}\\) is algebraic as a codimension-\\(r\\) class on \\(S^{r}\\). Consequently, rational HC holds in every degree of every power of \\(S\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Over an algebraic closure of \\(K\\), Lemma \\(4.1\\) gives a product of full special orthogonal groups in the totally real case, and a product of full general linear groups acting on standard and dual modules in the CM case.</p><p>The first fundamental theorem for special orthogonal groups says that invariant tensors are generated by the metric and a volume tensor. The corresponding theorem for general linear groups says that invariants are generated by standard–dual pairings; in the multilinear formulation, complete pair contractions span when the numbers of standard and dual slots agree. The spanning assertions have no stable-range restriction, as in the paper’s references [23] and [25].</p><p>Decompose each slot of \\(V^{\\otimes r}\\) by the algebraic eigenspace projectors. Lemmas \\(4.2\\) and \\(4.3\\), algebraic duality on \\(T\\), products, and permutations realize every invariant tensor. Scalar extension of invariants and Lemma \\(2.1\\) descend every rational Hodge tensor to a rational algebraic class.</p><p>Finally apply \\(\\pi_0,\\pi_4,\\pi_N,\\pi_T\\) to each factor of \\(S^m\\). The only non-Tate summand of \\(H^*(S,\\mathbf Q)\\) is \\(T\\) in degree two. A Künneth component with \\(r\\) transcendental slots, \\(a\\) divisor slots, and \\(b\\) top-degree slots has degree\n\\[\n2r+2a+4b.\n\\]\nThe constructed cycles have codimension \\(r+a+2b\\). They give every Hodge class in every degree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Section \\(2.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The Hodge structure \\(T\\) is simple.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be any projective K3 surface, \\(T=T_S\\), \\(V=T(1)\\), and \\(E=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(T)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Hodge structure \\(T\\) is simple. The algebra \\(E\\) is a number field, either totally real or CM. Its \\(q\\)-adjoint involution is respectively the identity or CM conjugation.</p><p>If \\(E\\) is totally real, there is a nondegenerate symmetric \\(E\\)-bilinear form \\(\\varphi\\), with \\(\\operatorname{Tr}_{E/\\mathbf Q}\\varphi=q\\), such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hg}(V)=\\operatorname{Res}_{E/\\mathbf Q}\\operatorname{SO}(T,\\varphi).\n\\]\nIf \\(E\\) is CM with real subfield \\(E^{+}\\), the corresponding form is Hermitian and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hg}(V)=\\operatorname{Res}_{E^{+}/\\mathbf Q}\\operatorname{U}(T,\\varphi).\n\\]</p><p>For a finite Galois field \\(K/\\mathbf Q\\) containing all embeddings of \\(E\\),\n\\[\nV_K=\\bigoplus_{\\eta:E\\hookrightarrow K}V_\\eta.\n\\]\nIn the totally real case the summands are orthogonal and nondegenerate. In the CM case \\(q\\) pairs \\(V_\\eta\\) perfectly with \\(V_{\\bar\\eta}\\) and trivially with all other summands.</p>", "proof": "<p>Semisimplicity splits every sub-Hodge structure of \\(T\\). At most one summand contains its one-dimensional \\((2,0)\\)-part. Every other summand would be of type \\((1,1)\\), contrary to the definition of \\(T\\) and Lefschetz’s \\((1,1)\\)-theorem. Thus \\(T\\) is simple.</p><p>Its endomorphism division algebra acts faithfully on \\(H^{2,0}(S)\\), and therefore embeds into \\(\\mathbf C\\); it is a number field. The field, adjoint involution, and group descriptions are Zarhin’s Hodge-group theorem, in the forms cited in the paper as [43], [38, Section \\(2.1\\) and Theorem \\(2.8\\)], and [41, Section \\(2.1\\)]. The eigenspace and pairing descriptions follow from the \\(E\\)-action and its adjoint.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every relative volume line \\( \\bigwedge^{d}V_\\eta\\subset(\\bigwedge^{d}V)_K \\) is algebraic with coefficients in \\(K\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition \\(4\\), and suppose \\(E\\) is totally real.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(d=\\dim_E T\\), and use the eigenspaces of Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every relative volume line\n\\[\n\\bigwedge^{d}V_\\eta\\subset(\\bigwedge^{d}V)_K\n\\]\nis algebraic with coefficients in \\(K\\). The inverse restricted metrics \\(q_\\eta^{-1}\\), the projectors \\(p_\\eta:V_K\\to V_\\eta\\), and every rational action \\(a\\in E\\) are algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each volume line is fixed by \\(\\operatorname{Hg}(V)_K\\). Invariants commute with scalar extension, so these lines lie in the scalar extension of the rational Hodge-class space of \\(\\bigwedge^{d}V\\). Proposition \\(2\\) algebraizes that space.</p><p>Choose \\(0\\ne\\alpha_\\eta\\in\\bigwedge^{d}V_\\eta\\). Contracting \\(\\alpha_\\eta\\otimes\\alpha_\\eta\\) by \\(q\\) in \\(d-1\\) pairs gives\n\\[\nc_\\eta q_\\eta^{-1}\\in V_\\eta\\otimes V_\\eta,\\qquad c_\\eta\\in K^*.\n\\]\nIn an orthogonal basis \\(u_i\\), with \\(q(u_i,u_i)=a_i\\ne0\\), the remaining tensor is a nonzero scalar multiple of\n\\[\n\\sum_i a_i^{-1}u_i\\otimes u_i;\n\\]\nits scalar contains the nonzero product \\(\\prod_i a_i\\).</p><p>These contractions are algebraic: intersect along the relevant diagonals and push forward on powers of \\(S\\). Their codimension is\n\\[\n2d-2(d-1)=2\n\\]\non \\(S^2\\). Pairing \\(q_\\eta^{-1}\\) with \\(q\\) gives \\(p_\\eta\\). For \\(a\\in E\\),\n\\[\na=\\sum_\\eta\\eta(a)p_\\eta.\n\\]\nThis class is rational, so the coefficient-descent identity of Lemma \\(2.1\\) proves rational algebraicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every element of \\(E\\), every eigenspace projector \\(p_\\eta\\) with coefficients in \\(K\\), and every separate standard–dual pairing tensor between \\(V_\\eta\\) and \\(V_{\\bar\\eta}\\) are algebraic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition \\(4\\), and suppose \\(E\\) is CM.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the eigenspaces \\(V_\\eta\\) of Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every element of \\(E\\), every eigenspace projector \\(p_\\eta\\) with coefficients in \\(K\\), and every separate standard–dual pairing tensor between \\(V_\\eta\\) and \\(V_{\\bar\\eta}\\) are algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>The norm-one elements span \\(E\\) over \\(\\mathbf Q\\). Their rational span \\(F\\) is a unital subalgebra closed under multiplication, hence a field. If \\(\\bar s=-s\\), then\n\\[\nu=\\frac{1+s}{1-s},\\qquad u\\bar u=1,\\qquad\ns=\\frac{u-1}{u+1}\\in F.\n\\]\nFixing a nonzero such \\(s\\), every \\(a\\in E^+\\) satisfies \\(as\\in F\\), hence \\(a=(as)/s\\in F\\). Thus \\(F=E\\).</p><p>Every norm-one element is a Hodge isometry of \\(T\\). Extend it by the identity on \\(\\operatorname{NS}(S)_{\\mathbf Q}\\). Buskin’s theorem algebraizes the resulting rational Hodge isometry of \\(H^2(S,\\mathbf Q)\\). Rational linear combinations and \\(\\pi_T\\) therefore give all endomorphisms in \\(E\\).</p><p>For a primitive element \\(b\\) of \\(E/\\mathbf Q\\),\n\\[\np_\\eta=\n\\prod_{\\tau\\ne\\eta}\n\\frac{b-\\tau(b)\\operatorname{id}}{\\eta(b)-\\tau(b)}\n\\]\nis algebraic with coefficients in \\(K\\). Applying \\(p_\\eta\\otimes p_{\\bar\\eta}\\) to the inverse metric gives the separate pairing tensor. No assumption that \\(\\dim_E T=1\\) is made.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are integers \\( 1\\le c\\le b,\\qquad 1\\le k_1,\\ldots,k_c\\le\\dim_{\\mathbf Q}V \\) and a surjective rational Hodge map \\( \\bigotimes_{j=1}^{c}\\bigwedge^{k_j}V\\longrightarrow H. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be any projective K3 surface, \\(V=T_S(1)\\), and \\(G=\\operatorname{Hg}(V)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a nontrivial rational simple representation occurring in a direct sum of tensor powers of \\(V\\), with its induced weight-zero Hodge structure.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose its Hodge level\n  \\[\n  \\max\\{|a-b|:H^{a,b}\\ne0\\}\n  \\]\n  is at most \\(2b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are integers\n\\[\n1\\le c\\le b,\\qquad\n1\\le k_1,\\ldots,k_c\\le\\dim_{\\mathbf Q}V\n\\]\nand a surjective rational Hodge map\n\\[\n\\bigotimes_{j=1}^{c}\\bigwedge^{k_j}V\\longrightarrow H.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(E=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(T_S)\\) and \\(a=\\dim_E T_S\\), and use Lemma \\(4.1\\). The multiset of constituents of \\(H\\) over an algebraic closure of \\(\\mathbf Q\\) is Galois invariant.</p><p>In the totally real case, \\(a\\ge3\\). Indeed,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{End}_G(V)=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(V)=E,\n\\]\nalso after scalar extension. If \\(a=1\\), the split group is trivial and its commutant has dimension \\(e^2&gt;e\\), where \\(e=[E:\\mathbf Q]=\\dim_{\\mathbf Q}V\\ge2\\). If \\(a=2\\), each standard \\(\\operatorname{SO}_2\\)-module is a sum of two distinct characters, giving commutant dimension at least \\(2e&gt;e\\). Both contradict the equality.</p><p>Over \\(\\mathbf C\\), write\n\\[\nG_{\\mathbf C}=\\prod_{\\sigma:E\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf C}\n\\operatorname{SO}(V_\\sigma,q_\\sigma).\n\\]\nOnly one distinguished embedding has nontrivial Hodge cocharacter. For a constituent of \\(H_{\\mathbf C}\\), let \\(w_\\sigma=\\lambda_{\\sigma,1}\\). Galois transports the actual standard eigenspace at \\(\\sigma\\) to that at the distinguished embedding, preserving the cocharacter class with vector weights \\(1,-1,0,\\ldots,0\\). Since the conjugate constituent still occurs in \\(H_{\\mathbf C}\\), Lemma \\(5.1\\) gives \\(w_\\sigma\\le b\\). In type \\(D_4\\), triality exchanging vector and half-spin modules cannot occur in this transport because it does not preserve the actual standard module.</p><p>Set \\(c=\\max_\\sigma w_\\sigma\\). Form signed orthogonal columns, padding with zero heights \\(h_{\\sigma j}\\), and put\n\\[\nk_j=\\sum_\\sigma h_{\\sigma j}.\n\\]\nThe decomposition of the exterior power of a direct sum contains\n\\[\n\\bigotimes_\\sigma\\bigwedge^{h_{\\sigma j}}V_\\sigma\n\\subset\\bigwedge^{k_j}V_{\\mathbf C}.\n\\]\nTaking Cartan components over columns yields the chosen constituent. Widths are maximized, not added.</p><p>In the CM case choose one embedding \\(\\eta\\) from each conjugate pair:\n\\[\nG_{\\mathbf C}=\\prod_\\eta\\operatorname{GL}(W_\\eta),\n\\qquad\nV_{\\mathbf C}=\\bigoplus_\\eta(W_\\eta\\oplus W_\\eta^*),\n\\qquad \\dim W_\\eta=a.\n\\]\nOn the distinguished standard factor the cocharacter is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(z,1,\\ldots,1)\\); its dual has inverse action. For highest weight\n\\[\n\\lambda_{\\eta,1}\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_{\\eta,a},\n\\]\nthe extrema are \\(\\lambda_{\\eta,1},\\lambda_{\\eta,a}\\). Galois transports standard modules to standard modules or their duals, replacing a weight in the latter case by\n\\[\n(-\\lambda_{\\eta,a},\\ldots,-\\lambda_{\\eta,1}),\n\\]\nwithout an additional central twist. Rationality and the level bound imply\n\\[\nw_\\eta=\\max\\{\\lambda_{\\eta,1},-\\lambda_{\\eta,a},0\\}\\le b.\n\\]\nSet \\(c=\\max_\\eta w_\\eta\\), and define\n\\[\nh_{\\eta j}^+=\\#\\{i:\\lambda_{\\eta,i}\\ge j\\},\\qquad\nh_{\\eta j}^-=\\#\\{i:\\lambda_{\\eta,i}\\le-j\\},\n\\qquad\nk_j=\\sum_\\eta(h_{\\eta j}^++h_{\\eta j}^-).\n\\]\nThe exterior decomposition contains\n\\[\n\\bigotimes_\\eta\n\\left(\\bigwedge^{h_{\\eta j}^+}W_\\eta\n\\otimes\\bigwedge^{h_{\\eta j}^-}W_\\eta^*\\right)\n\\subset\\bigwedge^{k_j}V_{\\mathbf C}.\n\\]\nThe sum of these signed column highest weights is exactly \\(\\lambda_\\eta\\), including its central character.</p><p>In either case, \\(c=0\\) would make a constituent trivial; a rational simple representation with a trivial complex constituent is trivial. Thus \\(c\\ge1\\). A factor attaining the maximum has a nonempty column for every \\(j\\), so \\(k_j\\ge1\\); also \\(k_j\\le\\dim_{\\mathbf Q}V\\).</p><p>Retain the full rational carrier \\(C_{\\mathbf k}\\). Sharing a complex constituent gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_G(C_{\\mathbf k},H)\\otimes\\mathbf C\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{G_{\\mathbf C}}\n((C_{\\mathbf k})_{\\mathbf C},H_{\\mathbf C})\\ne0.\n\\]\nA nonzero rational equivariant map is surjective and Hodge. No individual complex projector is assumed rational.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are integers \\(c,s\\ge1\\) and \\(1\\le k_i\\le t\\), with \\( c\\le b,\\qquad s=\\sum_i k_i, \\) and a surjective rational Hodge map \\( \\bigotimes_{i=1}^{c}\\bigwedge^{k_i}V\\longrightarrow H. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition \\(5\\), but assume \\(G=\\operatorname{SO}(V,q)\\) and \\(t=\\dim V\\ge3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are integers \\(c,s\\ge1\\) and \\(1\\le k_i\\le t\\), with\n\\[\nc\\le b,\\qquad s=\\sum_i k_i,\n\\]\nand a surjective rational Hodge map\n\\[\n\\bigotimes_{i=1}^{c}\\bigwedge^{k_i}V\\longrightarrow H.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose complex orthogonal torus coordinates in which the Hodge cocharacter has vector weights \\(1,0,\\ldots,0,-1\\). The integral highest weights of tensor representations are\n\\[\n\\lambda_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_l\\ge0\n\\quad(t=2l+1),\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n\\lambda_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_{l-1}\\ge|\\lambda_l|\n\\quad(t=2l).\n\\]\nHalf-integral spin weights do not occur in tensor powers of the standard module.</p><p>The largest cocharacter value is \\(\\lambda_1\\), by dominance and the convex hull of the Weyl orbit; the value \\(-\\lambda_1\\) also occurs. The Hodge level is therefore \\(2\\lambda_1\\).</p><p>Set \\(c=\\lambda_1\\). In odd dimension, take the columns of the partition \\((\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_l)\\). Their exterior highest-weight vectors have tensor product of highest weight \\(\\lambda\\). In even dimension, use the columns of\n\\[\n(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_{l-1},|\\lambda_l|).\n\\]\nFor columns of height \\(l\\), choose the highest-weight vector of the sign of \\(\\lambda_l\\), among the weights\n\\[\n(1,\\ldots,1,1),\\qquad(1,\\ldots,1,-1)\n\\]\nin the full middle exterior power. Retain both constituents in the carrier.</p><p>Choose a complex constituent \\(H_\\lambda\\) of \\(H_{\\mathbf C}\\). The resulting rational carrier \\(C_{\\mathbf k}=\\bigotimes_i\\bigwedge^{k_i}V\\) shares this constituent with \\(H_{\\mathbf C}\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\operatorname{SO}(V,q)}(C_{\\mathbf k},H)\\otimes\\mathbf C\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\operatorname{SO}(V_{\\mathbf C},q)}\n((C_{\\mathbf k})_{\\mathbf C},H_{\\mathbf C})\\ne0.\n\\]\nA nonzero rational map is surjective by rational simplicity and is Hodge. The same column argument includes \\(t=3\\) and \\(t=4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Section \\(2.6\\), the generalized Hodge assertion of Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(m,j,p\\), geometric coniveau \\(N^{p}H^{j}(S^{m},\\mathbf Q)\\) equals the largest rational sub-Hodge structure of Hodge coniveau at least \\(p\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) satisfy the exact even-Clifford algebraicity hypothesis of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(m,j,p\\), geometric coniveau \\(N^{p}H^{j}(S^{m},\\mathbf Q)\\) equals the largest rational sub-Hodge structure of Hodge coniveau at least \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(V=T_S(1)\\) and \\(G=\\operatorname{Hg}(V)\\). Proposition \\(4\\) gives HC for all powers of \\(S\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nH\\subset H^{2d}(S^{m},\\mathbf Q)\n\\]\nbe a simple rational sub-Hodge structure of Hodge coniveau at least \\(p\\), with \\(0\\le p\\le d\\). The Künneth decomposition shows that \\(H^{2d}(S^m,\\mathbf Q)(d)\\) is a direct sum of tensor powers of \\(V\\), since all other factors are Tate. Thus \\(H(d)\\) is a rational simple \\(G\\)-representation of level at most \\(2(d-p)\\).</p><p>If \\(H(d)\\) is trivial, ordinary HC and a finite basis put \\(H\\) in \\(N^{d}\\subset N^{p}\\). Otherwise Proposition \\(5\\) gives\n\\[\nf:C_{\\mathbf k}(s)\\twoheadrightarrow H(d),\\qquad\nC_{\\mathbf k}=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^{c}\\bigwedge^{k_i}T\n\\subset H^{2s}(S^{s},\\mathbf Q),\n\\]\nwhere \\(s=\\sum_i k_i\\) and \\(c\\le d-p\\).</p><p>Extend \\(f\\) by zero on a rational Hodge complement. Poincaré duality identifies it with a Hodge class of codimension \\(s+d\\) on \\(S^{s}\\times S^{m}\\). Proposition \\(4\\) gives\n\\[\nC_f\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{s+d}(S^{s}\\times S^{m})_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nrealizing it.</p><p>Take the exterior product of the returns of Proposition \\(2\\):\n\\[\nR_{\\mathbf k}\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{nc+s-c}(Z^{c}\\times S^{s})_{\\mathbf Q}.\n\\]\nIts image from \\(H^{2c}(Z^{c},\\mathbf Q)\\) contains \\(C_{\\mathbf k}\\), since the tensor product of the \\(F_{k_i}\\) is a right inverse there. Hence\n\\[\nC_fR_{\\mathbf k}\\in\n\\operatorname{CH}^{nc+d-c}(Z^{c}\\times S^{m})_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nhas image containing \\(H\\). Lemma \\(3.1\\) gives\n\\[\nH\\subset N^{d-c}H^{2d}(S^{m},\\mathbf Q)\n\\subset N^{p}H^{2d}(S^{m},\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nFor a nonsimple substructure, take the finite union of the supports for its simple summands. This treats actual occurrences, multiplicities, and diagonally embedded isomorphic summands.</p><p>Conversely, let \\(X=S^m\\), and let \\(B\\subset X\\) have codimension at least \\(p\\). For each irreducible component \\(B_i\\), choose a smooth projective resolution \\(\\widetilde B_i\\to B_i\\), let \\(q_i:\\widetilde B_i\\to X\\), and put \\(c_i=\\operatorname{codim}_X B_i\\ge p\\). Deligne’s exactness theorem for a proper smooth variety and closed support, as cited in [13, Proposition \\(8.2.7\\) and Corollary \\(8.2.8\\)], gives\n\\[\n\\ker\\!\\left(H^j(X,\\mathbf Q)\\to H^j(X\\setminus B,\\mathbf Q)\\right)\n=\n\\sum_i(q_i)_*H^{j-2c_i}(\\widetilde B_i,\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nEach Gysin map has source\n\\[\nH^{j-2c_i}(\\widetilde B_i,\\mathbf Q)(-c_i),\n\\]\nwhose two Hodge indices are at least \\(c_i\\ge p\\). Thus geometric coniveau has the required Hodge coniveau.</p><p>If \\(p&gt;d\\), this reverse inclusion forces \\(N^pH^{2d}=0\\). The cases \\(p\\le0\\) are immediate, and odd cohomology of \\(S^m\\) vanishes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a smooth double cover \\(Y\\to K=\\operatorname{Km}(B')\\) such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on a smooth finite-level chart of principally polarized abelian surfaces.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be indecomposable, let \\(M=\\mathcal O_B(H)\\) be a symmetric principal polarization, and choose \\(\\chi\\in\\operatorname{Pic}^{0}(B)[4]\\) of exact order four.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(d\\in H^0(B,M^2)\\) such that \\(D=(d=0)\\) is smooth and avoids \\(B[2]\\). On affine section charts normalize an actual section by a functional \\(\\ell(d)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\pi:B'\\to B,\\qquad\\pi_*\\mathcal O_{B'}=\\mathcal O_B\\oplus\\chi^2\n  \\]\n  be the degree-two isogeny. Choose a trivialization of \\(\\pi^*\\chi^2\\), set \\(L=\\pi^*(M\\chi)\\), and define\n  \\[\n  f:X=\\operatorname{Spec}_{B'}(\\mathcal O_{B'}\\oplus L^{-1})\\to B',\n  \\qquad z^2=\\pi^*d.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\delta\\) be the sheet involution, and let \\(j\\) be the lift of inversion defined by the normalized symmetric linearization of \\(L\\). Put\n  \\[\n  U_X=H^2(X,\\mathbf Q)^{j=1,\\delta=-1}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth double cover \\(Y\\to K=\\operatorname{Km}(B')\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The lattice\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=H^2(Y,\\mathbf Z)^{\\delta=-1}/\\mathrm{torsion}\n   \\]\n   is even, has signature \\((2,14)\\), and has 2-elementary discriminant group. For fixed \\((B,M,\\chi)\\), monodromy acts trivially on that group.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There are eight mutually orthogonal algebraic anti-invariant classes \\(a_i\\), with \\(a_i^2=-4\\), whose perpendicular\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal U=\\langle a_1,\\ldots,a_8\\rangle^\\perp\\subset\\mathcal V_{\\mathbf Q}\n   \\]\n   has signature \\((2,6)\\) and, with its \\(Y\\)-intersection form, is rationally isometric to \\(V_P\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Resolved pullback gives a flat Hodge isomorphism\n   \\[\n   \\beta:\\mathcal U\\xrightarrow{\\sim}U_X,\\qquad\n   (\\beta x,\\beta y)_X=2(x,y)_Y.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The algebraic projector\n   \\[\n   e=\\frac14(\\Delta_X+\\Gamma_j-\\Gamma_\\delta-\\Gamma_{j\\delta})\n   \\]\n   has image \\(U_X\\) in degree two and vanishes in every other degree.</p><p>All constructions are compatible with variation on the indicated smooth opens.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(7.1\\) and \\(7.2\\) give the cover, lattice properties, exceptional classes, and pullback comparison.</p><p>To determine the rational form, \\(H^+\\) contains \\(H^2(K,\\mathbf Z)(2)\\) with 2-power index. This pullback lattice has absolute determinant \\(2^{22}\\), a rational square. Gluing gives the same absolute determinant square class for \\(\\mathcal V\\). Its negative index is fourteen, so its determinant is positive. Splitting off the eight lines of square \\(-4\\) gives\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathcal U)=1\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbf Q^*/\\mathbf Q^{*2}.\n\\]\nAt every odd prime, \\((1\\pm\\delta)/2\\) split the unimodular lattice orthogonally, and the \\(a_i\\) also span a unimodular local lattice. Thus \\(\\mathcal U\\) admits a unimodular \\(\\mathbf Z_p\\)-lattice. The same is true for \\(T_P\\), whose determinant is \\(16\\). Both forms have Hasse invariant \\(1\\) at odd primes; their real Hasse invariants are\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\binom62}=-1.\n\\]\nHilbert reciprocity gives the same invariant \\(-1\\) at \\(2\\). Local classification and the rational local-to-global criterion identify \\(\\mathcal U\\) with \\(V_P\\).</p><p>The projector \\(e=(1+j)(1-\\delta)/4\\) has the asserted degree-two image. The sheet involution fixes \\(H^0,H^4\\), and fixes \\(H^1=f^*H^1(B')\\); by duality it fixes \\(H^3\\). Hence \\(e\\) vanishes outside degree two.</p><p>The covers, blowups, quotients, and graph correspondences are relative after the finite choices and restriction to smooth opens. Two normalized affine sections differing by a nonvanishing scalar are locally identified by a holomorphic square root, preserving the involutions and rational eigenspaces.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The parameter space has dimension six and contains a nonempty open of the stated smooth divisors.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the abelian data and covers of Proposition \\(7\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The parameter space has dimension six and contains a nonempty open of the stated smooth divisors. The normalized involution on \\(L\\) has eight positive and eight negative fiber signs on \\(B'[2]\\).</p><p>The local inversion quotients construct \\(Y\\to K\\), and\n\\[\nq(Y)=0,\\qquad p_g(Y)=2,\\qquad b_2(Y)=38.\n\\]\nIts invariant rational second cohomology has signature \\((3,19)\\), and its anti-invariant part has signature \\((2,14)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Ample vanishing and Riemann–Roch give\n\\[\nh^0(M\\chi)=h^0(M\\chi^{-1})=1,\\qquad h^0(M^2)=4,\n\\]\nwith no higher cohomology. The system \\(|M^2|\\) is basepoint-free: sums of opposite theta translates belong to it and can avoid any prescribed point. Its normalized symmetric linearization fixes all sections; the second-order theta basis is even. Bertini and avoidance of finitely many evaluation hyperplanes give the required nonempty open. The projective section fibers have dimension three over three-dimensional abelian data.</p><p>Write \\(B=\\mathbf C^2/\\Lambda\\), represent \\(\\chi\\) by \\(c:\\Lambda\\to\\mu_4\\), and put \\(\\Lambda'=\\ker(c^2)\\). Choose \\(\\lambda\\notin\\Lambda'\\); its class \\(k\\) generates \\(\\ker\\pi\\). Translation by \\(k\\) pairs the sixteen points of \\(B'[2]\\). The signs of \\(\\pi^*M\\) agree within a pair, while the flat factor changes by\n\\[\n\\frac{c(2v+2\\lambda)}{c(2v)}=c(2\\lambda)=-1.\n\\]</p><p>At a positive-sign point, \\(j\\) preserves the two sheets and acts by \\((u,v)\\mapsto(-u,-v)\\), giving two quotient nodes. Their resolutions give two disjoint \\((-2)\\)-curves over the base Kummer exceptional curve. At a negative-sign point, \\(j\\) exchanges the sheets; the quotient is smooth and locally maps as \\(\\mathbf C^2\\to\\mathbf C^2/\\{\\pm1\\}\\). Blowing up resolves this map and gives branching along that exceptional curve.</p><p>The divisor \\(D'=\\pi^{-1}D\\) is smooth, connected, and ample, with square \\(16\\), so \\(g(D')=9\\). Inversion acts freely, and its quotient has genus five. The branch of \\(Y\\to K\\) is this quotient together with eight disjoint exceptional curves. Hence\n\\[\ne(Y)=2e(K)-\\bigl((2-2\\cdot5)+8\\cdot2\\bigr)=40.\n\\]</p><p>The cover algebra and ample vanishing give\n\\[\nH^1(X,\\mathbf Q)=f^*H^1(B',\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nInversion acts by \\(-1\\), so \\(q(Y)=0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nH^{2,0}(X)=H^{2,0}(B')\\oplus H^0(B',L),\n\\quad\nH^0(B',L)=H^0(B,M\\chi)\\oplus H^0(B,M\\chi^{-1}).\n\\]\nInversion exchanges the last two one-dimensional summands, and the first summand is invariant under both involutions. Thus \\(p_g(Y)=2\\), \\(b_2(Y)=38\\), and the full signature is \\((5,33)\\). Pullback of \\(H^2(K,\\mathbf Q)\\), with form multiplied by two, is the invariant part; subtracting its signature gives \\((2,14)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The lattice \\(\\mathcal V\\) is even and 2-elementary.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(Y,\\mathcal V\\) of Proposition \\(7\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The lattice \\(\\mathcal V\\) is even and 2-elementary. At each positive-sign fixed point, if \\(E_i^+,E_i^-\\) are the two exceptional curves, then\n\\[\na_i=[E_i^+]-[E_i^-],\\qquad a_i^2=-4.\n\\]\nTheir span is flat, and its perpendicular is identified with \\(U_X\\) by resolved pullback, multiplying the form by two.</p>", "proof": "<p>The canonical class \\(K_Y\\) is invariant. For \\(v\\in\\mathcal V\\), \\(v\\cdot K_Y=0\\), and Wu’s characteristic parity formula gives \\(v^2\\equiv v\\cdot K_Y\\pmod2\\); hence \\(\\mathcal V\\) is even.</p><p>Let \\(H=H^2(Y,\\mathbf Z)/\\mathrm{torsion}\\) and \\(H^+\\) be its invariant lattice. Integral Poincaré duality makes \\(H\\) unimodular. Both eigensublattices are primitive, and\n\\[\n2x=(x+\\delta x)+(x-\\delta x).\n\\]\nThus \\(H/(H^+\\oplus\\mathcal V)\\) is killed by two. Unimodular gluing identifies this quotient with each discriminant group. For fixed \\(B'\\), pullback from the fixed Kummer surface fixes \\(H^+_{\\mathbf Q}\\) pointwise, hence its integral lattice and discriminant group; equivariant gluing gives trivial discriminant monodromy on \\(\\mathcal V\\).</p><p>The curves \\(E_i^\\pm\\) are exchanged by \\(\\delta\\), disjoint, and have square \\(-2\\). Their differences have the asserted intersections. Transport may permute indices or exchange labels, but preserves the span. Near passage of the branch through a positive-sign point, the labels are governed by a square root and \\(a_i\\) changes sign.</p><p>A common resolution of \\(X\\dashrightarrow Y\\) shows that the only added anti-invariant directions at positive-sign points are the \\(a_i\\); blowups at negative-sign points add only invariant directions. Pullback of their perpendicular is orthogonal to the exceptional classes over \\(X\\), hence comes from \\(H^2(X,\\mathbf Q)\\), with image \\(U_X\\). The degree-two projection formula multiplies the form by two.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The period map of \\(\\mathcal U\\), equivalently of \\(U_X\\), has rank six on a nonempty Zariski-open subset.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the six-dimensional family of Proposition \\(7\\), with rank-eight variation \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The period map of \\(\\mathcal U\\), equivalently of \\(U_X\\), has rank six on a nonempty Zariski-open subset. For a very general parameter, \\(\\mathcal U\\) is simple, has no nonzero rational \\((1,1)\\)-class, and has Hodge group \\(\\operatorname{SO}(\\mathcal U)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(8.2\\) gives rank six. The type-IV domain of signature \\((2,6)\\) has dimension six, so the period map is locally an isomorphism there. The rank condition is Zariski open because it is a rank condition on the algebraic Kodaira–Spencer map.</p><p>Hodge circles rotating oriented positive two-planes from any nonempty open generate a Zariski-dense subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{SO}(\\mathcal U)\\). A proper rational algebraic subgroup stabilizes a rational line in a tensor representation not stabilized by the full group: take a finite-dimensional representation of regular functions containing generators of its defining ideal, intersect with that ideal, and take the top exterior power. Semisimplicity places this representation in tensor powers and duals of \\(\\mathcal U(1)\\).</p><p>A connected compact Hodge circle stabilizing a rational line fixes it pointwise. Thus containment in a proper rational subgroup forces a specified rational tensor to have type \\((0,0)\\), a proper closed analytic Hodge condition. There are countably many rational tensors. Outside their proper loci, the Hodge group is full special orthogonal. Its standard representation is absolutely irreducible and has no invariants; simplicity and absence of rational \\((1,1)\\)-classes follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a flat complex identification of \\(U_X\\) with a character eigenspace having \\( U_X^{p,q}\\simeq H^q\\!\\left(B,\\Omega_B^p(\\log D)\\otimes M^{-1}\\chi\\right), \\qquad p+q=2. \\) In particular \\(U_X^{2,0}=H^0(B,M\\chi)\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition \\(7\\), and set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E=T_B(-\\log D).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a translation-invariant volume form \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(0\\ne\\theta\\in H^0(B,M\\chi)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a flat complex identification of \\(U_X\\) with a character eigenspace having\n\\[\nU_X^{p,q}\\simeq\nH^q\\!\\left(B,\\Omega_B^p(\\log D)\\otimes M^{-1}\\chi\\right),\n\\qquad p+q=2.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(U_X^{2,0}=H^0(B,M\\chi)\\) is one-dimensional. The period differential is induced on \\(H^1\\) by\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\xrightarrow{\\ \\theta\\ }\\mathcal E\\otimes M\\chi\n\\simeq\\Omega_B^1(\\log D)\\otimes M^{-1}\\chi.\n\\]\nThe Kodaira–Spencer map identifies the tangent space of the full family \\((B,M,\\chi,[d])\\) with \\(H^1(B,\\mathcal E)\\), of dimension six.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\widetilde B\\to B\\) be the cyclic degree-four isogeny trivializing \\(\\chi\\), with generator \\(g\\), and let \\(Z\\to B\\) be the untwisted double cover \\(w^2=d\\). On \\(\\widetilde X=Z\\times_B\\widetilde B\\),\n\\[\nX=\\widetilde X/\\langle\\delta g^2\\rangle.\n\\]\nOn sheet-anti-invariant cohomology, invariance under \\(\\delta g^2\\) selects \\(g\\)-eigenvalues \\(i,-i\\). Inversion exchanges them. Projection to either identifies their \\(j\\)-invariant part with that eigenspace. Choose the character convention giving \\(\\chi\\).</p><p>Locally, for \\(D=(x=0)\\) and \\(w^2=x\\), sheet-odd forms are generated by\n\\[\nw;\\qquad w\\,dy,\\ w\\,\\frac{dx}{x};\\qquad\nw\\,\\frac{dx}{x}\\wedge dy.\n\\]\nThe generator has transition rule \\(M^{-1}\\). Selecting the character tensors by \\(\\chi\\), giving the asserted eigensheaves. The logarithmic differential has local term \\(\\frac12d\\log x\\), together with the flat connection on \\(\\chi\\).</p><p>A logarithmic vector field satisfying \\(v(f)=af\\) lifts uniquely to\n\\[\nv+\\frac a2w\\partial_w.\n\\]\nContraction with the sheet-odd two-form gives multiplication by \\(\\theta\\). The bundle identification is\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\otimes\\mathcal O_B(D)\\simeq\\Omega_B^1(\\log D),\n\\qquad \\mathcal O_B(D)=M^2.\n\\]</p><p>The logarithmic normal sequence is\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal E\\to T_B\\to\\mathcal O_D(D)=K_D\\to0.\n\\]\nThere are no infinitesimal translations preserving \\(D\\), since the stabilizer of its ample bundle is finite. Hence\n\\[\n0\\to H^0(K_D)/H^0(T_B)\\to H^1(\\mathcal E)\n\\to\\ker\\!\\left(H^1(T_B)\\to H^1(K_D)\\right)\\to0.\n\\]\nHere \\(h^0(K_D)=5\\), \\(h^0(T_B)=2\\), \\(h^1(T_B)=4\\), and\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal O_B\\xrightarrow{d}M^2\\to K_D\\to0\n\\]\nidentifies \\(H^1(K_D)\\) with \\(H^2(\\mathcal O_B)\\). The boundary maps are contraction with \\(c_1(D)\\): in frames \\(e_j=g_{ij}e_i\\), \\(d=f_ie_i\\), the difference of local lifts is \\(v(\\log g_{ij})\\). The degree-one contraction is nonzero and has three-dimensional kernel, the polarized deformation space.</p><p>Extending \\(M\\) imposes the same condition since \\(c_1(M)=c_1(D)/2\\); the symmetric extension is unique because inversion acts by \\(-1\\) on the \\(H^1(\\mathcal O_B)\\)-torsor of extensions. Torsion and isogeny data extend uniquely.</p><p>For fixed \\(B\\), section directions \\(H^0(M^2)/\\langle d\\rangle\\) identify with \\(H^0(K_D)/H^0(T_B)\\), because contraction\n\\[\nH^0(T_B)\\longrightarrow H^1(\\mathcal O_B)\n\\]\nwith \\(c_1(D)\\) is an isomorphism. Ample vanishing and base change lift every polarized deformation to the section bundle. Thus the full Kodaira–Spencer map is an isomorphism and \\(h^1(\\mathcal E)=6\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The curve \\(F\\) is smooth of genus two, \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F)\\simeq B\\), and \\(\\eta\\) is nontrivial two-torsion.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Lemma \\(8.1\\), and let \\(F=(\\theta=0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(K=K_F\\) and \\(\\eta=\\chi^{-2}|_F\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The curve \\(F\\) is smooth of genus two, \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F)\\simeq B\\), and \\(\\eta\\) is nontrivial two-torsion. For general \\(d\\),\n\\[\nH^0\\!\\left(F,(\\mathcal E\\otimes M\\chi)|_F\\right)=0.\n\\]\nConsequently the period differential is injective on all six tangent directions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Indecomposability makes the theta curve smooth of genus two. An integral singular theta curve would have normalization of genus at most one and could not generate \\(B\\); a reducible theta divisor would give the product polarization. The restriction pairing on \\(H^1(B,\\mathbf Z)\\) is unimodular, so the induced Jacobian map is an isomorphism. Thus restriction on \\(\\operatorname{Pic}^0\\) is an isomorphism, and \\(\\eta\\ne\\mathcal O_F\\).</p><p>Adjunction gives\n\\[\n(M\\chi)|_F=K,\\qquad M^2|_F=K^{\\otimes2}\\otimes\\eta.\n\\]\nFor transverse \\(D\\),\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal E\\to\\mathcal E\\otimes M\\chi\n\\to(\\mathcal E\\otimes M\\chi)|_F\\to0.\n\\]\nPut \\(Z=D\\cap F\\), a reduced divisor of degree four. The last sheaf’s global sections are the kernel of\n\\[\nA_d:H^0(F,K)^{\\oplus2}\\to\nH^0\\!\\left(Z,(K^{\\otimes3}\\otimes\\eta)|_Z\\right),\n\\qquad v\\mapsto(v\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}dd)|_Z,\n\\]\nbetween four-dimensional spaces.</p><p>Restriction \\(H^0(B,M^2)\\to H^0(F,K^2\\eta)\\) is onto with kernel generated by \\(\\theta\\theta'\\), where \\(0\\ne\\theta'\\in H^0(B,M\\chi^{-1})\\). Its restriction \\(t=\\theta'|_F\\) is a nonzero section of \\(K\\eta\\). Choose a general \\(s=d|_F\\) with simple zeros avoiding those of \\(t\\); \\(K^2\\eta\\) is basepoint-free.</p><p>Replacing \\(d\\) by \\(d+\\lambda\\theta\\theta'\\) replaces \\(A_d\\) by \\(A_d+\\lambda A'\\). For translation fields \\(v_1,v_2\\), the normal derivatives\n\\[\n\\theta_i=(v_i\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}d\\theta)|_F\n\\]\nform a basis of \\(H^0(K)\\), and\n\\[\nA'(a,b)=t(a\\theta_1+b\\theta_2)|_Z.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\operatorname{Sym}^2H^0(K)\\simeq H^0(K^2)\\) and \\(K^2(-Z)\\simeq\\eta\\) has no section,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}A'=tH^0(K^2)|_Z,\\qquad\\operatorname{rank}A'=3.\n\\]\nIts kernel is spanned by \\(k=(\\theta_2,-\\theta_1)\\), a nowhere-zero section of \\(T_F\\otimes K\\simeq\\mathcal O_F\\). In the adjunction convention \\(\\operatorname{Res}(\\Omega/h)\\), this section is the constant\n\\[\nc=-\\Omega(v_1,v_2)\\ne0,\n\\]\nso \\(A_d(k)=c\\,d_Fs|_Z\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\n0\\to\\eta\\xrightarrow{\\cdot s}K^2\\to K^2|_Z\\to0,\n\\]\nthe connecting class of \\((d_Fs)/t|_Z\\) pairs with \\(t\\in H^0(K\\eta)\\). Locally write\n\\[\ns=f(u)(du)^2\\epsilon,\\qquad t=g(u)du\\,\\epsilon,\\qquad \\epsilon^2=1.\n\\]\nThe associated principal part pairs to \\((f'/f)du\\), whence\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\partial((d_Fs)/t|_Z),t\\right\\rangle\n=\\sum_{z\\in Z}\\operatorname{Res}_z\\frac{df}{f}=4\n\\]\nup to the fixed nonzero Serre-duality normalization. Thus \\(A_d(\\ker A')\\not\\subset\\operatorname{im}A'\\).</p><p>The coefficient of \\(\\lambda^3\\) in \\(\\det(A_d+\\lambda A')\\) is nonzero. Hence \\(A_d\\) is invertible for general \\(d\\). Intersect this nonempty open with smooth divisors avoiding two-torsion. The exact sequence above and Lemma \\(8.1\\) give the asserted injectivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every parameter in a nonempty Zariski-open subset, there are smooth projective connected curves \\(C_1,C_2\\) and a dominant generically finite morphism \\( g:C_1\\times C_2\\longrightarrow X. \\) If \\(U_X\\) has no …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the abelian-cover family \\(X\\) of Proposition \\(7\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every parameter in a nonempty Zariski-open subset, there are smooth projective connected curves \\(C_1,C_2\\) and a dominant generically finite morphism\n\\[\ng:C_1\\times C_2\\longrightarrow X.\n\\]\nIf \\(U_X\\) has no nonzero rational \\((1,1)\\)-class, an algebraic correspondence induces an injection\n\\[\nU_X\\hookrightarrow H^1(C_1,\\mathbf Q)\\otimes H^1(C_2,\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nIn particular, this injection exists at a very general parameter.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose connected components \\(C_1^0\\subset C_+\\), \\(C_2^0\\subset C_-\\). Pull back \\(B'\\to B\\) separately and choose connected components \\(C_i'\\to C_i^0\\), giving lifts \\(a_i':C_i'\\to B'\\). Their addition\n\\[\nF'=a_1'+a_2':C_1'\\times C_2'\\to B'\n\\]\nis finite and surjective. Lemma \\(9.4\\) gives\n\\[\nF'^*(\\pi^*D)=2E.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(S'=C_1'\\times C_2'\\), put\n\\[\nN=F'^*L\\otimes\\mathcal O_{S'}(-E),\\qquad\nu=F'^*(\\pi^*d)/s_E^2\\in H^0(S',N^2).\n\\]\nThe section \\(u\\) is nowhere zero. The étale cover\n\\[\n\\widetilde S'=\\operatorname{Spec}_{S'}(\\mathcal O_{S'}\\oplus N^{-1})\n\\]\nis the normalization of \\(S'\\times_{B'}X\\) and maps to it everywhere: locally \\(z^2=uf^2\\) normalizes to \\(y^2=u\\), with \\(z=fy\\), also at singularities and intersections of \\(E\\).</p><p>Its character\n\\[\n\\rho:\\pi_1(C_1')\\times\\pi_1(C_2')\\to\\mathbf Z/2\n\\]\nis the sum of its two factor restrictions. Take the connected finite étale covers \\(C_i\\to C_i'\\) killing those restrictions. The pulled-back double cover is trivial. A section gives \\(g:C_1\\times C_2\\to X\\). Its composite to \\(B'\\) is finite surjective, so \\(g\\) is dominant and generically finite; \\(X\\) is irreducible because its nonempty reduced branch gives a nontrivial function-field square class.</p><p>The projection formula gives \\(g_*g^*=(\\deg g)\\operatorname{id}\\). If \\(U_X\\) has no rational \\((1,1)\\)-class, it has no nonzero Hodge map to \\(\\mathbf Q(-1)\\). Thus its pullback has zero projection to the two outer Künneth summands of \\(H^2(C_1\\times C_2)\\). With points \\(p_i\\in C_i\\), put\n\\[\n\\pi_1^{C_i}=\\Delta_{C_i}-[p_i\\times C_i]-[C_i\\times p_i].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n(\\pi_1^{C_1}\\boxtimes\\pi_1^{C_2})\\circ{}^t\\Gamma_g\\circ e\n\\]\nis the required codimension-two algebraic injection. Proposition \\(8\\) supplies the Hodge condition very generally.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a nonempty Zariski-open subset, \\(D\\) has no nontrivial translation stabilizer.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(B,D\\) of Proposition \\(7\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a nonempty Zariski-open subset, \\(D\\) has no nontrivial translation stabilizer. Its quotient\n\\[\nC=D/\\{\\pm1\\}\n\\]\nis smooth of genus three, and the connected étale double cover \\(D\\to C\\) is defined by a nontrivial \\(\\epsilon\\in\\operatorname{Pic}^0(C)[2]\\). The Gauss map is\n\\[\nD\\longrightarrow C\\xrightarrow{|K_C\\otimes\\epsilon|}\\mathbf P^1,\n\\]\nof degrees two and four. No fiber of the degree-four map has all multiplicities even.</p><p>The good open is nonempty on every connected component obtained by adjoining the finite theta, torsion, and level choices defining \\(X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Ample vanishing in the divisor sequence makes \\(D\\) connected, hence irreducible. Adjunction gives \\(g(D)=5\\). Inversion acts freely, so \\(g(C)=3\\), and connectedness makes \\(\\epsilon\\) nontrivial.</p><p>The curve \\(D\\) generates \\(B\\); otherwise it would be an elliptic curve of square zero. Invariant one-forms on \\(B\\) therefore restrict injectively, span the two-dimensional anti-invariant space\n\\[\nH^0(C,K_C\\epsilon),\n\\]\nand have no common zero. They give the asserted basepoint-free degree-four pencil and its Gauss interpretation.</p><p>The six-dimensional family maps algebraically to the irreducible six-dimensional moduli space \\(\\mathcal R_3\\) of genus-three curves with nonzero two-torsion. Its fibers are countable: a fixed \\((C,\\epsilon)\\) fixes \\(D\\); maps to abelian surfaces are homomorphisms from \\(J(D)\\) followed by translations. Their kernels and isogeny choices are countable, translations respecting inversion satisfy \\(2b=\\mathrm{constant}\\), and polarization and level choices are discrete or finite. Countable finite-type fibers have dimension zero, so the map is dominant on each source component. The Torelli theorem similarly makes the Jacobian map from \\(\\mathcal R_3\\) dominant onto the irreducible six-dimensional space \\(\\mathcal A_3[\\epsilon\\ne0]\\).</p><p>For a theta characteristic \\(\\kappa\\), let\n\\[\n\\Theta_\\kappa=\\{\\alpha\\in J(C):h^0(C,\\kappa\\alpha)&gt;0\\}.\n\\]\nAn all-even member of \\(|K_C\\epsilon|\\) exists exactly when a half \\(2\\alpha=\\epsilon\\) lies on \\(\\Theta_\\kappa\\). The condition is independent of \\(\\kappa\\), is closed after a finite theta-level cover, and descends. It is proper: on a product of three elliptic curves choose \\(\\epsilon\\) nonzero in every factor. Its halves have order-four coordinates, while a symmetric product theta divisor is a union of coordinate divisors at two-torsion points. No half lies on it. Dominance therefore gives the desired nonempty open.</p><p>Finite torsion-level components surject onto the connected base. Finally a translation preserving \\(D\\) belongs to \\(B[2]\\). Every nonzero element acts nontrivially on \\(\\mathbf P H^0(M^2)\\), by the nondegenerate theta-group commutator pairing. Avoid its finitely many proper fixed loci.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an algebraic map \\(h:\\mathscr S\\to B\\) satisfying \\( 2h(s)=\\sum_{p\\in s}p,\\qquad h(s[p])=h(s)-p. \\) The selections split globally into degree-eight parity covers \\( \\mathscr S_+\\sqcup\\mathscr S_-. \\) Their …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix data in the open of Lemma \\(9.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R\\subset\\mathbf P^1\\) be the regular-value open of the Gauss pencil, with local fibers\n  \\[\n  \\{\\pm x_1,\\ldots,\\pm x_4\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A selection contains one point from each opposite pair. Let \\(\\mathscr S\\to R\\) be the degree-sixteen selection cover, and write \\(s[p]\\) for replacing \\(p\\) by \\(-p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an algebraic map \\(h:\\mathscr S\\to B\\) satisfying\n\\[\n2h(s)=\\sum_{p\\in s}p,\\qquad h(s[p])=h(s)-p.\n\\]\nThe selections split globally into degree-eight parity covers\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_+\\sqcup\\mathscr S_-.\n\\]\nTheir smooth projective completions \\(C_\\pm\\), possibly disconnected, carry extensions \\(a_\\pm:C_\\pm\\to B\\). Wherever the differential is nonzero, it is tangent to the Gauss direction recorded by the parameter.</p><p>For selections \\(s,t\\) over the same regular value,\n\\[\nh(s)+h(t)=\\sum_{p\\in s\\cap t}p,\n\\qquad h(s^c)=-h(s).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A coherent assignment on one fiber is determined by one selection \\(s_0\\) and one half \\(b\\) of its sum:\n\\[\nh(s)=b-\\sum_{p\\in s_0\\setminus s}p.\n\\]\nSuch assignments form an algebraic finite étale torsor under the constant group \\(B[2]\\). Construct it from pairs \\((s,b)\\) satisfying \\(2b=\\sum_{p\\in s}p\\), identifying \\((s,b)\\) with \\((s[p],b-p)\\). Local flips commute and are involutions. Monodromy acts on this torsor by translations.</p><p>Around an omitted value, choose at the central fiber one point from each opposite pair and take the entire nearby cluster specializing to it. This gives a monodromy-invariant selection. Its sum extends over the disc and has a holomorphic half because multiplication by two is étale. Thus local monodromy fixes one coherent assignment, and a torsor translation with a fixed point is the identity. The torsor extends étale over \\(\\mathbf P^1\\) and is trivial.</p><p>The parity double cover is likewise locally fixed and extends trivially over \\(\\mathbf P^1\\). Properness extends \\(h\\) to \\(C_\\pm\\). Differentiating gives\n\\[\nda_\\pm=\\frac12\\sum_{i=1}^4\\sigma_i\\,dx_i,\\qquad\\sigma_i\\in\\{\\pm1\\},\n\\]\nin the common Gauss direction.</p><p>The exact sum identity follows from coherence:\n\\[\nh(s)+h(t)=2h(s)-\\sum_{p\\in s\\setminus t}p.\n\\]\nChanging the assignment by \\(b_0\\in B[2]\\) changes this sum by \\(2b_0=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "No signed sum of a nonempty subset of the four local branches \\(x_i\\) is constant on a regular-value disc.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Lemma \\(9.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>No signed sum of a nonempty subset of the four local branches \\(x_i\\) is constant on a regular-value disc. Every connected component of \\(C_+\\) and \\(C_-\\) maps nonconstantly to \\(B\\). No image component from \\(C_+\\) is an opposite translate of one from \\(C_-\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A one-term relation contradicts nonconstancy of a branch. A two-term relation identifies open subsets of \\(D\\) with a translate of \\(D\\) or \\(-D=D\\); trivial translation stabilizer then identifies distinct opposite pairs, impossible.</p><p>A three-term relation continues, by transitivity on the four unsigned pairs, to four relations omitting each index. The coefficient matrix has zero diagonal and entries \\(\\pm1\\) elsewhere. Modulo two it is \\(J-I\\), whose square is the identity in dimension four. Its determinant is odd, hence nonzero, so the corresponding homomorphism \\(B^4\\to B^4\\) is an isogeny. The moving branches would lie in a fixed finite fiber.</p><p>Two nonopposite four-term sign relations differ in one to three positions; subtraction gives a forbidden proper partial relation. Thus a four-term relation determines its selection up to complement. Monodromy preserves that selection or exchanges it with its complement. By Lemma \\(9.1\\), every local multiplicity partition has a singleton, giving fixed signed points; hence local monodromy cannot exchange the selection with its opposite. Local monodromies generate the whole group, so four signed sheets would form a global invariant subset of the connected cover \\(D\\to\\mathbf P^1\\), impossible.</p><p>A constant component of \\(a_\\pm\\) gives a four-term relation. If image components satisfy \\(Z_+=b-Z_-\\), choose general paired smooth points and lift local inverse branches. Their tangent directions agree, so their Gauss parameters agree. Opposite-parity selections agree in one or three signed points. The sum identity of Lemma \\(9.2\\) then gives a forbidden partial relation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\(F\\) is finite on every product of connected components, has total degree eight, and \\( F^*D=2E_0 \\) for an effective integral Cartier divisor \\(E_0\\) on each such product.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(C_\\pm,a_\\pm\\) of Lemma \\(9.2\\), and define\n  \\[\n  F:C_+\\times C_-\\to B,\\qquad F(p,q)=a_+(p)+a_-(q).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(F\\) is finite on every product of connected components, has total degree eight, and\n\\[\nF^*D=2E_0\n\\]\nfor an effective integral Cartier divisor \\(E_0\\) on each such product.</p>", "proof": "<p>For either parity,\n\\[\n\\sum_\\sigma\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=8\\delta_{ij}.\n\\]\nFor an invariant real two-form \\(\\omega\\), the differential formula gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\in\\mathscr S_\\pm}h(s)^*\\omega\n=2\\sum_{i=1}^4x_i^*\\omega,\n\\]\nthe sum over the eight branches of \\(D\\). Integration yields\n\\[\n(a_+)_*[C_+]=(a_-)_*[C_-]=[D].\n\\]\nThis includes all component degrees.</p><p>A positive-dimensional addition fiber would project surjectively to both connected factors, forcing opposite-translate image curves, contrary to Lemma \\(9.3\\). Properness makes \\(F\\) finite, and each component product surjects onto \\(B\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\deg F=[D]^2=(2H)^2=8.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a signed point \\(x\\) in a regular Gauss fiber. There are four selections \\(s\\in\\mathscr S_+\\) containing \\(x\\). For\n\\[\n\\tau=s^c[-x]\\in\\mathscr S_-,\n\\]\ncoherence gives \\(h(s)+h(\\tau)=x\\). These four local curves in the source map isomorphically to an open of \\(D\\). Both factor differentials are tangent to \\(D\\), so addition has rank at most one there. In local coordinates,\n\\[\nF(u,v)=(u,G(u,v)),\\qquad G(u,0)=G_v(u,0)=0,\n\\]\nso each branch has multiplicity at least two in \\(F^*D\\).</p><p>Write \\(F^*D=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha E_\\alpha\\), and let\n\\[\nf_\\alpha=[\\mathbf C(E_\\alpha):\\mathbf C(D)].\n\\]\nFiniteness and the valuation degree formula give\n\\[\n\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha f_\\alpha=8.\n\\]\nThe four constructed branches already contribute at least eight, so they exhaust the pullback and all have multiplicity exactly two. Smoothness of the source makes the half-divisor Cartier.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a smaller normalized product chart \\(\\mathcal A\\times Q\\) of abelian data and affine section coordinates, lying in the simultaneous geometric open of Propositions \\(8\\) and \\(9\\), with choices available in this …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A residual set has complement a countable union of closed nowhere-dense subsets.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in a nonempty regular period chart of the abelian-cover family.</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribe countably many proper closed analytic period loci.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smaller normalized product chart \\(\\mathcal A\\times Q\\) of abelian data and affine section coordinates, lying in the simultaneous geometric open of Propositions \\(8\\) and \\(9\\), with choices available in this order:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. abelian data \\(a\\) in a residual subset of \\(\\mathcal A\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. after \\(a\\) is fixed, an actual section pencil in a residual subset of each prescribed nonempty admissible pencil open meeting \\(Q\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. after that pencil is fixed, all but countably many points of its intersection with \\(Q\\) and a nonempty algebraic good open.</p><p>These points avoid the prescribed loci. The pencils have\n\\[\nd_t=d_0+td_1,\\qquad\\ell(d_0)=1,\\qquad\\ell(d_1)=0,\n\\]\nwith \\(d_1\\) smooth and avoiding two-torsion. Proper marked rational Hodge-tensor conditions have only countably many exceptional parameters on the whole connected smooth part of the pencil. Further pencil-specific countable exceptions may be added after the pencil is fixed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a datum in the simultaneous geometric open and the period chart, and an independent good section \\(d_\\infty\\) in the same fiber. Trivialize the section bundle and choose \\(\\ell(d_*)=1,\\ell(d_\\infty)=0\\). After shrinking, \\(\\mathbf P(\\ker\\ell)\\) contains good sections in every fiber. Choose a normalized product polydisc \\(\\mathcal A\\times Q\\) on which the period map is locally an isomorphism.</p><p>Affine lines are the stated pencils. Good directions form a nonempty dense Zariski open, and the direction map on lines meeting \\(Q\\) is a surjective submersion. Every pencil meeting \\(Q\\) has only finitely many geometric bad parameters and is nonconstant in period, since the period differential is injective on section directions.</p><p>Pull back the prescribed loci to \\(H_m\\subset\\mathcal A\\times Q\\). The sets\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_m=\\{a:\\{a\\}\\times Q\\subset H_m\\}\n\\]\nare closed and have empty interior by the identity theorem. Outside their union, each fiber \\(H_{m,a}\\) is proper analytic.</p><p>For fixed such \\(a\\), the sets\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m,a}=\\{\\Lambda:\\Lambda\\cap Q\\subset H_{m,a}\\}\n\\]\nare closed and nowhere dense in the space of affine lines meeting \\(Q\\). Closedness follows by limits of points on lines; an open set of lines sweeps out an open set of \\(Q\\). Baire’s theorem leaves a residual set of pencils. Each proper analytic condition then has discrete, hence countable, intersection with the pencil disc.</p><p>Include all proper rational tensor Hodge loci in the initial list. Continue markings on the universal cover of the connected smooth pencil. A tensor condition holding on an open would hold on the chosen disc by analytic continuation and would have been excluded. Countably many tensors, continuation choices, and charts therefore give only countably many exceptional parameters. Intersect with the dense open of good directions, and add any later pencil-specific countable exceptions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a nonempty algebraic open \\(O\\subset\\mathbf A_t^1\\) and a smooth projective K3 family \\( S^0\\to O \\) with a flat isometric embedding \\( \\mathcal U\\hookrightarrow R^2(S^0/O)_*\\mathbf Q. \\) Very generally, this …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose abelian data and a section pencil \\(d_t=d_0+td_1\\) in the generic sets of Proposition \\(10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal V\\) and \\(\\mathcal U\\) be the integral anti-invariant lattice and rank-eight rational variation of Proposition \\(7\\), using the \\(Y\\)-cup form on \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a nonempty algebraic open \\(O\\subset\\mathbf A_t^1\\) and a smooth projective K3 family\n\\[\nS^0\\to O\n\\]\nwith a flat isometric embedding\n\\[\n\\mathcal U\\hookrightarrow R^2(S^0/O)_*\\mathbf Q.\n\\]\nVery generally, this summand is the full transcendental part.</p><p>For every \\(s\\) in a nonempty Zariski open of \\(\\mathbf A_s^1\\), every smooth projective model of the total family obtained by\n\\[\nt=s+u^2\n\\]\nis uniruled. The K3 family is defined over an open of the original \\(t\\)-line; no additional unspecified finite base change remains in this conclusion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(7\\) gives trivial discriminant monodromy. Apply Lemma \\(11.1\\) and use the period line of \\(\\mathcal V\\) in the extended K3 lattice. Rationally,\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3},\\mathbf Q}\n=\n\\mathcal U\\oplus\\langle a_1,\\ldots,a_8\\rangle_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\oplus\\mathcal M_{\\mathbf Q}^{\\perp}.\n\\]\nThe last fourteen dimensions are of type \\((1,1)\\). By the generic choices of Proposition \\(10\\) and Proposition \\(8\\), \\(\\mathcal U\\) has no rational \\((1,1)\\)-classes very generally. A persistent rational root has flat \\(\\mathcal U\\)-projection remaining \\((1,1)\\), hence zero very generally and identically. Lemmas \\(11.2\\) and \\(11.4\\) therefore give \\(S^0\\) on the original line. Lefschetz’s theorem identifies its very general transcendental part with \\(\\mathcal U\\). No integral Néron–Severi lattice for this auxiliary K3 is specified.</p><p>Let \\(\\Delta\\) contain the finite critical values and the complement of the chosen open. For \\(s\\notin\\Delta\\), the substitution \\(t=s+u^2\\) is unramified over finite critical values and has branch at the smooth value \\(s\\). At infinity it kills the sign twist. Every remaining local monodromy is a product of integral root reflections perpendicular to \\(h\\) and the limiting period. Lemma \\(11.4\\) gives a flat polarized ADE family over \\(\\mathbf P_u^1\\).</p><p>Its Hodge line has degree one. The compactified upstairs double cover over \\(B'\\times\\mathbf P_u^1\\) uses \\(L\\boxtimes\\mathcal O(1)\\), with branch\n\\[\n(d_0+sd_1)u_0^2+d_1u_1^2.\n\\]\nThe anti-sheet direct image of relative canonical forms is\n\\[\nH^0(B',L)\\otimes\\mathcal O(1),\n\\]\nand its \\(j=+1\\) line is \\(\\mathcal O(1)\\). With \\(v=1/u\\), \\(w=z/u\\),\n\\[\nw^2=d_1+(d_0+sd_1)v^2,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\theta_+dx\\wedge dy}{z}\n=\nv\\frac{\\theta_+dx\\wedge dy}{w}.\n\\]\nThus the finite-chart frame has one zero at infinity. Lemma \\(11.2\\) shows that finite nodal parameters contribute neither an integral zero or pole nor a fractional inertia weight. Lemma \\(11.3\\) preserves the exact linearization, so \\(\\lambda\\simeq\\mathcal O(1)\\). Hence\n\\[\nK_{\\mathcal Z}=f^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-1),\n\\]\nand Lemma \\(11.5\\) applies.</p><p>Finally identify this model’s function field with the actual square pullback of the fixed \\(S^0\\)-family. Let \\(K=\\mathbf C(t)\\), \\(K_s=\\mathbf C(u)\\), and let \\(\\widetilde K/K\\) be the finite Galois cover used in the original construction. Form \\(\\widetilde K_s=\\widetilde K K_s\\). Its group over \\(K_s\\) is the corresponding subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(\\widetilde K/K)\\). A ramification index \\(e\\) under base-change index \\(d\\) becomes \\(e/\\gcd(e,d)\\), divisible by the pulled-back monodromy order \\(m/\\gcd(m,d)\\). Thus the common cover is admissible.</p><p>Functoriality through \\(q\\) identifies the new upstairs analytic family and group action with the literal pullback of the original algebraic ADE family. Fully faithful GAGA algebraizes this identification. Uniqueness of finite Galois descent identifies the new generic contraction with the original generic contraction tensored to \\(K_s\\). The fixed generic smooth K3 resolves that contraction, and its base change remains birational to it. Therefore the uniruledness applies to every smooth projective model of the fixed square-pulled-back family.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are an even finite-index overlattice \\(\\mathcal M\\) of \\(\\mathcal V\\), a primitive embedding \\(\\mathcal M\\hookrightarrow\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\), and a primitive positive vector \\(h\\in\\mathcal M^\\perp\\), such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal V\\) be an even lattice of signature \\((2,14)\\) with elementary 2-primary discriminant group.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\subset O(\\mathcal V)\\) act trivially on that group.</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite abelian group \\(A\\), let \\(\\ell(A)\\) be its minimum number of generators.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are an even finite-index overlattice \\(\\mathcal M\\) of \\(\\mathcal V\\), a primitive embedding \\(\\mathcal M\\hookrightarrow\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\), and a primitive positive vector \\(h\\in\\mathcal M^\\perp\\), such that \\(G\\) extends to \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\) as the identity on \\(\\mathcal M^\\perp\\).</p><p>Integral \\((-2)\\)-roots of \\(\\mathcal V\\) remain integral roots, and products of their reflections extend as the same products of K3 root reflections.</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(A_{\\mathcal V}\\simeq\\mathbf F_2^n\\), let \\(\\beta=2b\\) be the nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form. Its diagonal kernel \\(E\\) has dimension at least \\(n-1\\), and \\(\\beta|_E\\) is alternating. The inverse image of a Lagrangian of its symplectic quotient is isotropic of dimension\n\\[\n\\frac{\\dim E+\\dim\\operatorname{rad}(\\beta|_E)}2\n\\ge\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}2\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nOn it, the discriminant quadratic form is an \\(\\mathbf F_2\\)-valued linear functional. If \\(n\\ge5\\), there is a nonzero isotropic vector. Adjoining it gives an even overlattice with discriminant form \\(H^\\perp/H\\), reducing length by two. Repeat to obtain \\(\\ell(A_{\\mathcal M})\\le4\\). Every enlargement is \\(G\\)-stable, with trivial discriminant action.</p><p>Nikulin’s sufficient embedding criterion applies because\n\\[\n3&gt;2,\\quad19&gt;14,\\quad22-16=6\\ge\\ell(A_{\\mathcal M})+2.\n\\]\nThe complement has signature \\((1,5)\\), hence a primitive positive integral vector \\(h\\). Unimodular gluing extends each action by the identity on the complement.</p><p>A root \\(r\\in\\mathcal V\\) remains integral, and\n\\[\ns_r(x)=x+(x,r)r\n\\]\nis integral on each enlargement and on \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\), fixing the complement. This identifies extensions of products.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At every finite singular parameter, monodromy on \\(\\mathcal V\\) is a product of reflections in mutually orthogonal integral \\((-2)\\)-roots.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the general original-line pencil of Proposition \\(11\\), with smooth leading section avoiding two-torsion.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every finite singular parameter, monodromy on \\(\\mathcal V\\) is a product of reflections in mutually orthogonal integral \\((-2)\\)-roots. These roots are perpendicular to the limiting Hodge line. After killing the finite monodromy, periods extend to interior periods. At infinity monodromy is \\(-\\operatorname{id}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(|2H|\\)-map realizes the inversion quotient as the quartic Kummer surface. A general line in its dual meets discriminant components transversely at either a simple smooth-locus tangency or a hyperplane through one Kummer node, avoiding higher-contact and intersection loci of codimension at least two. Upstairs, these give exchanged ordinary nodes or a transverse ordinary-node smoothing at two-torsion. General pencil base points remain smooth.</p><p>At an exchanged pair, the local quotient double surface is\n\\[\nz^2=xy+t.\n\\]\nIts vanishing sphere is an integral anti-invariant \\((-2)\\)-root.</p><p>At a positive-sign fixed point, use the equivariant model\n\\[\nz^2=x^2+y^2+t,\\qquad j(x,y,z)=(-x,-y,z).\n\\]\nOn the resolved base chart \\(a=x^2,v=y/x\\),\n\\[\nz^2=a(1+v^2)+t.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=0\\), the exceptional branch and residual branch meet at \\(v=\\pm i\\), giving two disjoint node smoothings and two orthogonal anti-invariant roots.</p><p>At a negative-sign fixed point,\n\\[\nj(x,y,z)=(-x,-y,-z)\n\\]\nacts antipodally on the vanishing \\(S^2\\), so no class survives in the \\(j\\)-invariant quotient. The quotient of \\((0,0,\\pm\\sqrt t)\\) is one smooth point. Its blowup adds a \\(\\delta\\)-invariant class. Explicitly, with \\(\\eta=xz\\),\n\\[\n\\eta^2=a\\bigl(a(1+v^2)+t\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe ramification curve over \\(a=0\\) has square \\(-1\\), and is invariant. This event contributes no anti-invariant monodromy.</p><p>There is no further monodromy. In\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{\\mathbf Q}\n=\n\\mathcal U\\oplus\\langle a_1,\\ldots,a_8\\rangle_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\]\nPicard–Lefschetz on \\(X\\) gives one negative dimension per exchanged pair and one per positive fixed point on \\(U_X\\); the affected exceptional-difference line adds one more at each positive point. The orthogonal roots found above span precisely this negative eigenspace. Thus the operator is their reflection product.</p><p>After a quadratic local base change, resolve each \\(A_1\\) smoothing simultaneously. For\n\\[\nxy=(z-v)(z+v),\n\\]\nblow up \\((x,z-v)\\). The two charts have smooth coordinates \\((x,a,v)\\) and \\((b,y,v)\\), with\n\\[\nz=v+xa,\\quad y=a(2v+xa),\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nz=by-v,\\quad x=b(by-2v).\n\\]\nThe form \\(dx\\wedge dz/x\\) becomes \\(dx\\wedge da\\), and its equal expression \\(-dy\\wedge dz/y\\) becomes \\(db\\wedge dy\\); the resolution is crepant.</p><p>For nonzero \\(\\theta_+\\in H^0(B',L)^{j=+1}\\), the Hodge line of \\(U_X\\) has frame\n\\[\n\\frac{\\theta_+\\,dx\\wedge dy}{z}.\n\\]\nIt extends regularly and remains a nonzero global form on the resolved central surface, as checked away from the branch and the zero divisor of \\(\\theta_+\\). Its norm is positive. Flat involutions extend on cohomology and preserve the limiting filtration. The original frame is single-valued, so inertia fixes its limiting vector; the negative eigenspace is perpendicular to it. The exceptional-difference classes remain algebraic.</p><p>At infinity, \\(q_\\infty=t^{-1}\\) gives\n\\[\nz^2=q_\\infty^{-1}(d_1+q_\\infty d_0).\n\\]\nAfter adjoining \\(\\sqrt{q_\\infty}\\) and rescaling \\(z\\), the family extends smoothly. The only twist is \\(\\delta\\), which acts by \\(-\\operatorname{id}\\) on \\(\\mathcal V\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a morphism \\( q:\\mathcal P_h\\longrightarrow\\mathcal M_h \\) whose composition with the period map of smooth quasi-polarized K3 surfaces is canonical polarized contraction.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a primitive positive \\(h\\in\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal D_h\\) be a component of\n  \\[\n  \\{[\\sigma]\\in\\mathbf P(h^\\perp_{\\mathbf C}):\n  (\\sigma,\\sigma)=0,\\ (\\sigma,\\bar\\sigma)&gt;0\\},\n  \\]\n  and let \\(\\Gamma_h\\) be the integral isometries fixing \\(h\\) and preserving this component.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\mathcal P_h=[\\Gamma_h\\backslash\\mathcal D_h]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal M_h\\) be the moduli stack of primitively polarized ADE K3 surfaces of degree \\((h,h)\\), with polarizations understood as relative Picard classes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a morphism\n\\[\nq:\\mathcal P_h\\longrightarrow\\mathcal M_h\n\\]\nwhose composition with the period map of smooth quasi-polarized K3 surfaces is canonical polarized contraction. The pullback of the Hodge line is the tautological period line, including its \\(\\Gamma_h\\)-linearization.</p><p>At a period \\([\\sigma]\\), the kernel on stabilizers is the Weyl group \\(W_\\sigma\\) generated by reflections in\n\\[\nR_\\sigma=\\{r\\in\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}:\nr^2=-2,\\ (r,h)=0,\\ (r,\\sigma)=0\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Unimodular gluing identifies \\(\\Gamma_h\\) with the stable orthogonal group of \\(h^\\perp\\) preserving the component. The period-stack and contraction morphism are those of Di Lorenzo–Fringuelli–Vistoli [16, Lemma \\(1.12\\)], using their representable surjective étale period map from the smooth quasi-polarized stack.</p><p>On a local étale period chart, contraction identifies relative two-forms away from exceptional curves. In the full smooth family the exceptional locus has codimension at least two, so this line-bundle identification extends. Fiberwise it is the crepant ADE identification. On the relation space of two quasi-polarized lifts, the identifications agree on the dense ample locus by Torelli and therefore everywhere. Thus the full Hodge-line linearization descends.</p><p>For a root \\(r\\), its reflection fixes the period vector on its mirror. Very generally there, the rational Picard space is \\(\\mathbf Qh\\oplus\\mathbf Qr\\). The induced polarized automorphism lifts to the resolution, fixes \\(h\\) and the effective sign of \\(r\\), and is symplectic. Simplicity of the transcendental Hodge structure forces it to act identically there; Torelli makes it the identity. Separatedness extends this identity over the mirror, so all root reflections are killed.</p><p>Conversely, an integral Hodge isometry fixing \\(h\\) takes the ample chamber to another chamber whose closure contains \\(h\\). The Weyl group of roots perpendicular to \\(h\\) acts transitively on these chambers. After composing with such a Weyl element, strong Torelli realizes the isometry by an automorphism of the resolution. If its contraction is the identity, the lift is the identity on the dense nonexceptional locus and hence everywhere. The original isometry lies in \\(W_\\sigma\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a normal projective model \\(f:\\mathcal Z\\to C\\) whose restriction to \\(C^\\circ\\) is a polarized ADE K3 family realizing these integral period data modulo contracted roots.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a smooth projective complex curve, \\(D\\subset C\\) finite, and \\(C^\\circ=C\\setminus D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a K3-lattice local system on \\(C^\\circ\\), with primitive flat positive section \\(h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal F\\subset\\mathcal L\\otimes\\mathcal O_{C^\\circ}\\) be a holomorphic period line satisfying\n  \\[\n  (\\mathcal F,\\mathcal F)=0,\\quad\n  (\\sigma,\\bar\\sigma)&gt;0,\\quad\n  (\\mathcal F,h)=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose local monodromies are finite and, after local covers killing them, the period has an interior limit.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a normal projective model \\(f:\\mathcal Z\\to C\\) whose restriction to \\(C^\\circ\\) is a polarized ADE K3 family realizing these integral period data modulo contracted roots.</p><p>If each local monodromy belongs to the Weyl group generated by limiting roots\n\\[\nr^2=-2,\\qquad(r,h)=0,\\qquad(r,\\mathcal F_c)=0,\n\\]\nthen \\(\\mathcal Z\\to C\\) may be chosen flat with ADE K3 fibers everywhere. Its extended Hodge line descends to a line bundle \\(\\lambda\\) on \\(C\\), and\n\\[\n\\omega_{\\mathcal Z/C}\\simeq f^*\\lambda,\\qquad\nK_{\\mathcal Z}=f^*(K_C\\otimes\\lambda).\n\\]</p><p>Independently of this Weyl condition, suppose a nondegenerate rational sub-local system \\(\\mathcal U\\subset\\mathcal L_{\\mathbf Q}\\) contains \\(\\mathcal F\\), and every locally flat root remaining of type \\((1,1)\\) on an open is perpendicular to \\(\\mathcal U\\). After shrinking a nonempty algebraic open in \\(C^\\circ\\), there is a smooth projective K3 family \\(S^0\\) and a flat isometric realization of \\(\\mathcal U\\) in \\(R^2S^0_*\\mathbf Q\\), without changing the base curve.</p>", "proof": "<p>The marking torsor and period map give \\(C^\\circ\\to\\mathcal P_h\\); monodromy lies in \\(\\Gamma_h\\) because it fixes \\(h\\), has trivial restricted discriminant action by gluing, and preserves the period component. Pullback through Lemma \\(11.3\\) gives an analytic ADE family with compatible descent data.</p><p>Choose a finite Galois cover \\(\\widetilde C\\to C\\) whose ramification indices above \\(D\\) are divisible by the monodromy orders. Such a cover is obtained by an \\(N\\)-th root of a function with prescribed simple zeros at \\(D\\), allowing auxiliary branch points elsewhere. The pulled-back local system and period extend; functoriality through \\(q\\) supplies a compact analytic ADE family and its Galois action.</p><p>The family is normal by flatness and normality of the fibers. Local representatives of a sufficiently divisible polarizing class differ on overlaps by pullbacks of base line bundles: their ratios are fiberwise trivial, and cohomology and base change with \\(H^1(\\mathcal O)=0\\) identify evaluation. The remaining scalar obstruction lies in\n\\[\nH^2(\\widetilde C,\\mathcal O_{\\widetilde C}^*)=0,\n\\]\nby the exponential sequence, \\(H^2(\\mathcal O_{\\widetilde C})=0\\), and \\(H^3(\\widetilde C,\\mathbf Z)=0\\). Thus a relatively ample line bundle glues. A high power embeds the family in a projective bundle. GAGA algebraizes the vector bundle, ideal, and finite group action. Tensoring translates of an ample bundle gives a linearized ample bundle and a projective finite quotient. This quotient is the required normal projective model, with original descent over \\(C^\\circ\\).</p><p>At a Weyl puncture, write \\(t=w^m\\). Lemma \\(11.3\\) makes inertia act identically on the entire central ADE surface. For a completed local ring \\(A\\), with \\(g(w)=\\zeta w\\) and \\(g=\\operatorname{id}\\) modulo \\(w\\), put \\(B=A^{\\langle g\\rangle}\\). Reynolds averaging makes \\(B\\to A/(w)\\) onto. Successive division by \\(w\\) in character eigenspaces gives\n\\[\nB\\cap wA=w^mB,\\qquad B/(t)\\simeq A/(w),\n\\]\nand, for \\(A_j=\\{a:g(a)=\\zeta^ja\\}\\),\n\\[\nA_j=w^jB,\\qquad\nA=\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{m-1}w^jB,\\qquad\nB\\otimes_{\\mathbf C[[t]]}\\mathbf C[[w]]\\simeq A.\n\\]\nFaithful flatness of completion gives these assertions locally. The quotient is flat, its central fiber is the original reduced ADE surface, and no multiple fiber occurs.</p><p>To descend the primitive polarization, choose a representative line bundle on a small invariant disc. A semilinear lift \\(T\\) satisfies \\(T^m=c(t)\\operatorname{id}\\). Taking an \\(m\\)-th root of the invariant unit \\(c^{-1}\\) makes \\(T^m=\\operatorname{id}\\). On the connected proper reduced central fiber, \\(T\\) is a scalar character; adjust it to the identity. Averaging a local generator gives an invariant generator, so invariant transition functions descend an invertible sheaf whose central restriction is the primitive polarization. Scalar-cocycle gluing and GAGA complete the descent.</p><p>The Hodge line is treated separately: inertia fixes its limiting vector because every generating reflection is perpendicular to it. Averaging a nonzero local frame gives an invariant nonvanishing frame. This descends the prescribed Hodge character to \\(\\lambda\\). A flat family with Gorenstein ADE fibers is Gorenstein; \\(\\omega_{\\mathcal Z/C}\\) is invertible, its direct image is a line bundle commuting with base change, and evaluation is an isomorphism on every fiber. Lemma \\(11.3\\) identifies this direct image with \\(\\lambda\\), proving the canonical formula.</p><p>For the last assertion, resolve the generic ADE surface over \\(\\mathbf C(C)\\). Over a finite Galois extension, minimal resolutions descend by their unique isomorphisms over the surface; a norm of a relatively ample bundle gives projective descent. Spread this resolution over a smaller original open.</p><p>For a minimal resolution \\(\\pi:S\\to Z\\), with exceptional span \\(E_Z\\),\n\\[\n\\pi^*:H^2(Z,\\mathbf Q)\\hookrightarrow H^2(S,\\mathbf Q),\n\\qquad\\operatorname{im}\\pi^*=E_Z^\\perp.\n\\]\nThis follows from the resolution cohomology sequence, \\(H^1\\) of exceptional trees being zero, and nondegeneracy of the ADE intersection matrix. After shrinking, the corresponding sheaves are local systems.</p><p>Local smooth period lifts contract to the same family. Their exceptional roots are persistent, hence perpendicular to \\(\\mathcal U\\). Therefore their copies of \\(\\mathcal U\\) lie in the contraction cohomology and map isometrically to the spread resolution. On overlaps, marking discrepancies lie in the Weyl kernel of Lemma \\(11.3\\), which fixes \\(\\mathcal U\\). The maps glue.</p><p>Root mirrors perpendicular to \\(h\\) are locally finite: on a compact period set, roots perpendicular to the associated positive three-plane have bounded norm in a fixed positive-definite metric. Compactness of the finite cover then leaves only finitely many intersections with nonpersistent mirrors. Remove these and the finitely many spreading exceptions. No base change remains.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.5", "orig": "Corollary \\(5.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every smooth projective model of the function field of \\(\\mathcal Z\\) is uniruled.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the Weyl-condition case of Lemma \\(11.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  \\deg(K_C\\otimes\\lambda)&lt;0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every smooth projective model of the function field of \\(\\mathcal Z\\) is uniruled.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(p:\\widetilde{\\mathcal Z}\\to\\mathcal Z\\) be a smooth projective resolution and \\(A\\) sufficiently ample on \\(\\mathcal Z\\). Since \\(\\mathcal Z\\) is normal and Gorenstein, \\(K_{\\widetilde{\\mathcal Z}}-p^*K_{\\mathcal Z}\\) is exceptional. Each exceptional divisor has zero intersection with \\((p^*A)^2\\), by the projection formula, or by choosing two general members whose intersection avoids its codimension-at-least-two image. Therefore\n\\[\nK_{\\widetilde{\\mathcal Z}}\\cdot(p^*A)^2\n=\n\\deg(K_C\\otimes\\lambda)\\,(A|_{\\mathcal Z_c})^2&lt;0.\n\\]\nThe divisor \\(p^*A\\) is nef; effective, and hence pseudo-effective, divisors have nonnegative intersection with its square. Thus the canonical class is not pseudo-effective. Boucksom–Demailly–Păun–Peternell [6, Corollary \\(0.3\\)] gives uniruledness. This is birationally invariant.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The Hodge structure \\(H^3(Z)\\) is a direct summand of a finite sum of spaces \\( H^1(C_i)(-1) \\) for smooth projective curves \\(C_i\\), with both maps of the splitting induced by algebraic correspondences.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be a smooth projective uniruled threefold over \\(\\mathbf C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Hodge structure \\(H^3(Z)\\) is a direct summand of a finite sum of spaces\n\\[\nH^1(C_i)(-1)\n\\]\nfor smooth projective curves \\(C_i\\), with both maps of the splitting induced by algebraic correspondences.</p><p>Consequently every rational Hodge map between third cohomologies of two smooth projective uniruled threefolds is algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Uniruledness gives a generically finite dominant rational map \\(T\\times\\mathbf P^1\\dashrightarrow Z\\), for a smooth projective surface \\(T\\), after a generically finite change of rational-curve parameter surface if necessary. Principalize its base ideal by smooth projective blowups to obtain\n\\[\na:\\widetilde{T\\times\\mathbf P^1}\\to Z.\n\\]\nThe identity \\(a_*a^*=(\\deg a)\\operatorname{id}\\) gives an algebraic splitting.</p><p>The product and blowup formulas give\n\\[\nH^3(T\\times\\mathbf P^1)=H^3(T)\\oplus H^1(T)(-1).\n\\]\nA curve blowup adds \\(H^1(C)(-1)\\), and a point adds nothing. These decompositions have algebraic projectors. For a curve center, with exceptional inclusion \\(j:E\\hookrightarrow\\widetilde Z\\) and \\(q:E\\to C\\), inclusion is \\(j_*q^*\\), retraction is \\(-q_*j^*\\), because\n\\[\nq_*j^*j_*q^*y\n=q_*\\bigl(c_1(\\mathcal O_E(-1))\\cup q^*y\\bigr)=-y.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a smooth ample curve \\(i:C\\hookrightarrow T\\). Hard Lefschetz makes \\(i_*i^*\\) an isomorphism \\(H^1(T)(-1)\\to H^3(T)\\). Polarizability supplies a Hodge retraction of \\(i^*\\) and a Hodge section of \\(i_*\\). Poincaré duality identifies their tensors with rational \\((1,1)\\)-classes in\n\\[\nH^1(C)\\otimes H^1(T),\\qquad H^1(T)\\otimes H^1(C).\n\\]\nLefschetz’s theorem represents them by divisors. Together with restriction, Gysin, and point/fundamental classes on \\(\\mathbf P^1\\), these algebraically split both surface terms through curve \\(H^1(-1)\\).</p><p>A Hodge map between finite sums of these curve structures is represented by divisor correspondences on products of curves. Composition with the splittings gives the prescribed codimension-three correspondence between threefolds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Theorem \\(6.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a nonempty Zariski open \\(G\\subset O\\) and a countable \\(E\\subset G\\) such that, for every \\(b\\in G\\setminus E\\), there is \\( \\Theta_b\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S_b\\times X_b)_{\\mathbf Q} \\) inducing exactly …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(O\\subset\\mathbf A_t^1\\) be nonempty Zariski open, and let\n  \\[\n  f:\\mathscr S\\to O,\\qquad g:\\mathscr X\\to O\n  \\]\n  be smooth projective families of connected surfaces, with K3 fibers for \\(f\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal U\\subset R^2f_*\\mathbf Q\\) be a polarized K3-type subvariation with nondegenerate cup form, nonconstant period, and simple fibers outside a countable set.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(j,\\delta\\) be commuting involutions of \\(\\mathscr X/O\\), and suppose\n  \\[\n  e=\\frac14(1+j)(1-\\delta)\n  \\]\n  annihilates \\(R^qg_*\\mathbf Q\\) for \\(q\\ne2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an isomorphism of rational Hodge variations\n  \\[\n  \\varphi:\\mathcal U\\xrightarrow{\\sim}eR^2g_*\\mathbf Q.\n  \\]\n  It is not required to preserve cup forms.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(s\\) in a nonempty Zariski open, both families pulled back by \\(t=s+u^2\\) have smooth projective uniruled threefold models.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a nonempty Zariski open \\(G\\subset O\\) and a countable \\(E\\subset G\\) such that, for every \\(b\\in G\\setminus E\\), there is\n\\[\n\\Theta_b\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S_b\\times X_b)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\ninducing exactly \\(\\varphi_b\\) on \\(\\mathcal U_b\\). The open \\(G\\) and auxiliary correspondences used before choosing \\(b\\) can be fixed for the given pencil.</p>", "proof": "<p>The proof proceeds with the first models and their correspondence fixed before \\(b\\).</p><p>First, the fixed-part theorem gives\n\\[\nH^0(O,\\mathcal U)=0.\n\\]\nA nonzero invariant part would be a constant Hodge subvariation; at a simple fiber it would equal the whole fiber and make the period constant. For\n\\[\nI_s=\\{u\\in\\mathbf A^1:s+u^2\\in O\\},\\qquad s\\in O,\n\\]\nthe pulled-back monodromy image is the original image: a loop around the smooth branch value \\(s\\) changes cover parity but has trivial \\(\\mathcal U\\)-monodromy. Hence\n\\[\nH^0(I_s,\\mathcal U)=0.\n\\]</p><p>Lemma \\(13.1\\) gives the canonical original-curve splitting\n\\[\nRf_*\\mathbf Q\n\\simeq\\mathbf Q\\oplus(R^2f_*\\mathbf Q)[-2]\\oplus\\mathbf Q(-2)[-4],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\neRg_*\\mathbf Q\\simeq(eR^2g_*\\mathbf Q)[-2]\\simeq\\mathcal U[-2].\n\\]\nLet \\(p:R^2f_*\\mathbf Q\\to\\mathcal U\\) be orthogonal projection. Pull these coefficient projections back under both substitutions; no new surface-base splitting or algebraicity of \\(p\\) is required.</p><p>Use Lemma \\(13.3\\) for both families and shrink \\(G\\subset O\\) so that \\(Z_G^S,Z_G^X\\) are smooth projective with uniruled threefold fibers. Restriction and coefficient projection give\n\\[\nr_s^S:H^3(Z_s^S)\\to H^1(I_s,\\mathcal U),\\qquad\nr_s^X:H^3(Z_s^X)\\to H^1(I_s,\\mathcal U).\n\\]\nTransport the mixed Hodge structure from \\(eH^3(E_s^X)\\). Its lowest-weight part\n\\[\nT_s=W_3H^1(I_s,\\mathcal U)\n\\]\nis pure polarizable, and restriction makes \\(r_s^X\\) onto \\(T_s\\). Lemma \\(13.2\\), for \\(a=\\varphi p\\), gives a Hodge map \\(c_s\\) satisfying\n\\[\nr_s^Xc_s=r_s^S.\n\\]\nProposition \\(12\\) algebraizes \\(c_s\\).</p><p>The punctures of \\(I_s\\) are roots of \\(u^2=c-s\\), for \\(c\\in\\mathbf A^1\\setminus O\\), together with infinity; over \\(G\\subset O\\) they do not collide. Thus the \\(H^1(I_s,\\mathcal U)\\) and the maps \\(r^S,r^X\\) are local systems.</p><p>Parameterize all rational codimension-three cycles on \\(Z_G^S\\times_G Z_G^X\\) by countably many finite products of relative Hilbert schemes and rational coefficient lists. On connected smooth strata, fundamental Betti classes are flat: a finite locally free resolution of the universal flat structure sheaf gives a Chern character whose degree-six part restricts to the fundamental cycle class. Consequently\n\\[\nr^XC_*=r^S\n\\]\nholds everywhere or nowhere on each stratum. Successful strata cover \\(G\\). A nondominating finite-type stratum has finite image, so countably many cannot cover \\(G(\\mathbf C)\\). Choose a successful dominating stratum and a generically finite multisection; after normalization and shrinking it is finite étale \\(\\nu:G'\\to G\\), of degree \\(d\\). The trace\n\\[\nC=\\frac1d\\nu_*C'\\in\\operatorname{CH}^3(Z_G^S\\times_GZ_G^X)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nsatisfies the equality. Shrink for flat cycle representatives. Fix \\(G,Z_G^S,Z_G^X,C\\).</p><p>Now choose \\(b\\in G\\), subject to the later countable exceptions, and set \\(s=b+v^2\\). Lemma \\(13.3\\) gives \\(M^S,M^X\\), preserving\n\\[\nY^k=\\mathscr Y^k\\times_OQ_b\n\\]\nand the first-model open over \\(V_0\\). Their intersection is over\n\\[\nQ_b^\\circ=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbf A^2:v\\in V_0,\\ b+u^2+v^2\\in O\\}.\n\\]\nPull \\(C\\) to \\(V_0\\) and close it in \\(M^S\\times_{\\mathbf P_v^1}M^X\\). As a cycle in the absolute product this is\n\\[\n\\widehat C\\in\\operatorname{CH}^4(M^S\\times M^X)_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\]\nsupported over the diagonal of the \\(v\\)-line, with the required open relative action.</p><p>At \\(t=b\\), the conic is \\(L_+\\cup L_-\\), with \\(u=\\pm iv\\). Let \\(F_b^S=S_b,F_b^X=X_b\\). The complete cylinders \\(F_b^k\\times L_\\pm\\subset Y^k\\subset M^k\\) define\n\\[\nD_k\\in\\operatorname{CH}^3(F_b^k\\times M^k)_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\qquad\nd_k=(i_{k,+})_*p_{k,+}^*-(i_{k,-})_*p_{k,-}^*.\n\\]\nThey act \\(H^2(F_b^k)(-1)\\to H^4(M^k)\\). Since\n\\[\nL_+\\cdot L_-=1,\\qquad L_\\pm^2=-1\n\\]\n(their sum is the fiber divisor of \\(t-b\\)), self- and excess-intersection formulas give\n\\[\n{}^td_kd_k=-4\\operatorname{id}.\n\\]</p><p>The coefficient projection sends a cylinder Gysin class to the Gysin class of its coefficient. Explicitly, for a smooth divisor \\(\\sigma:D\\hookrightarrow B_2\\) mapped constantly to \\(b\\in O\\), and \\(x\\in\\mathcal U_b\\), the image is\n\\[\n\\sigma_*x\\in H^2(B_2,\\tau^*\\mathcal U).\n\\]\nThis follows from the natural coefficient Thom morphism\n\\[\n\\sigma_*\\sigma^*K[-2]\\simeq(\\sigma_*\\mathbf Q_D[-2])\\otimes K\\to K.\n\\]\nThe normal-disc calculation gives purity \\(\\sigma^!K\\simeq\\sigma^*K[-2]\\), naturally in every bounded locally constant complex \\(K\\). Proper base change and the projection formula identify total-space Gysin with this coefficient Gysin. Restriction of the original splitting to \\(D\\to b\\) is the canonical point splitting. This proves compatibility for both families and for each line separately.</p><p>For \\(x\\in\\mathcal U_b\\), the restriction\n\\[\nc=d_S(x)|_{Z_G^S\\times_GV_0}\n\\]\nvanishes on every projective threefold fiber. Each surface Gysin term pairs with a degree-two class whose restriction is monodromy invariant; its \\(\\mathcal U\\)-projection vanishes by \\(H^0(I_s,\\mathcal U)=0\\). Poincaré duality proves the vanishing. Thus\n\\[\nc\\in H^1(V_0,\\mathcal H^3A_S)\\subset\\mathbb H^4(V_0,A_S),\n\\qquad A_k=R\\rho_{k*}\\mathbf Q.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nK=Rq_*\\tau^*\\mathcal U[-2],\n\\]\nthe coefficient maps \\(\\beta_S:A_S\\to K\\), \\(\\beta_X:A_X\\to K\\), and the relative action \\(\\gamma:A_S\\to A_X\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^3(\\beta_X\\gamma)=\\mathcal H^3(\\beta_S).\n\\]\nLemma \\(13.1\\) applied to the canonical \\(H^1(\\mathcal H^3)\\)-term yields equality on \\(c\\). The Thom compatibility identifies its coefficient with\n\\[\n\\sigma_{+*}x-\\sigma_{-*}x.\n\\]\nSince the entire \\(e\\)-part is \\(\\mathcal U[-2]\\), this proves\n\\[\ne\\,\\widehat C_*d_S(x)=d_X(\\varphi_bx)\n\\]\non \\(\\mathscr X\\times_OQ_b^\\circ\\).</p><p>To remove the boundary error, put \\(F=\\mathbf A^1\\setminus G\\), fixed before \\(b\\). The complement in \\(Y^X\\) is a disjoint union of:</p><p class=\"li\">two infinity sections, each carrying the fixed family \\(\\mathscr X\\to O\\);</p><p class=\"li\">for each \\(c_0\\in F\\) and each root \\(a_0^2=c_0-b\\), the divisor \\(v=a_0\\), carrying\n  \\[\n  E_{c_0}^X=\\{(x,u):g(x)=c_0+u^2\\in O\\}.\n  \\]\nFinite divisors have no points at infinity and are mutually disjoint. Localization gives an exact sequence of mixed Hodge structures\n\\[\nH^2(D)(-1)\\to H^4(Y^X)\\to\nH^4(\\mathscr X\\times_OQ_b^\\circ).\n\\]\nThe first term’s weight-four part is \\(H_F(-1)\\), where\n\\[\nH_F=\n(W_2H^2(\\mathscr X))^{\\oplus2}\n\\oplus\n\\bigoplus_{c_0\\in F}(W_2H^2(E_{c_0}^X))^{\\oplus2}\n\\]\nis fixed and pure polarizable.</p><p>For all but countably many \\(b\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(\\mathcal U_b,H_F)=0.\n\\]\nExclude nonsimple fibers. On countably many marked discs there are countably many rational injections into \\(H_F\\). Each being Hodge fixes the entire source filtration, and a nonconstant period curve meets that fixed filtration discretely.</p><p>The error map from \\(\\mathcal U_b(-1)\\), pure of weight four, lies in the localization kernel. Weight strictness puts it in the Gysin image of \\(H_F(-1)\\). Semisimplicity lifts it to \\(H_F(-1)\\), and the displayed Hom vanishing kills it. Therefore equality holds throughout \\(Y^X\\).</p><p>Finally the complete transpose cylinders can be evaluated there. They commute with \\(e\\) on the surface coordinate. Set\n\\[\n\\Theta_b=-\\frac14e_b\\circ{}^tD_X\\circ\\widehat C\\circ D_S.\n\\]\nIts codimension is \\(3+4+3-4-4=2\\), and\n\\[\n(\\Theta_b)_*x\n=-\\frac14{}^td_Xd_X(\\varphi_bx)=\\varphi_bx.\n\\]\nThe excluded \\(b\\)-values are countable and were chosen only after fixing all first data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A bounded complex whose cohomology sheaves occur only in even degrees has a unique splitting into its shifted cohomology sheaves inducing their identity maps.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a smooth affine complex curve.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in the rational derived category of sheaves with finite-rank local-system cohomology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A bounded complex whose cohomology sheaves occur only in even degrees has a unique splitting into its shifted cohomology sheaves inducing their identity maps.</p><p>For any bounded complex \\(A\\) with local-system cohomology, there is a natural exact sequence\n\\[\n0\\to H^1(B,\\mathcal H^{n-1}A)\\to\\mathbb H^n(B,A)\n\\to H^0(B,\\mathcal H^nA)\\to0.\n\\]\nA morphism’s action on the left-hand term is determined by its action on \\(\\mathcal H^{n-1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An affine complex curve has the homotopy type of a one-dimensional CW complex. For local systems \\(L,M\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}_B^r(L,M)\n=\nH^r(B,L^\\vee\\otimes M)=0\\qquad(r&gt;1).\n\\]\nFor distinct nonzero even degrees \\(i&lt;j\\), the splitting obstruction lies in \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^{j-i+1}\\), and ambiguity in \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^{j-i}\\); both vanish. Induction gives uniqueness.</p><p>The hypercohomology spectral sequence has only columns zero and one. Its degeneration and functoriality give the exact sequence and the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a rational Hodge map \\( c:H^3(Z^S)\\to H^3(Z^X) \\) such that \\( e\\,\\operatorname{res}_X(cz) = H^1(I,a)(\\operatorname{res}_S z). \\) The open source and projected target are canonically their \\(H^1(R^2)\\)-terms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I\\) be smooth affine, with a smooth projective K3 family \\(h_S:E^S\\to I\\) and a smooth projective surface family \\(h_X:E^X\\to I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let a relative algebraic idempotent \\(e\\) annihilate \\(R^qh_{X*}\\mathbf Q\\) for \\(q\\ne2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  a:R^2h_{S*}\\mathbf Q\\to eR^2h_{X*}\\mathbf Q\n  \\]\n  be a morphism of Hodge variations.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z^S,Z^X\\) be smooth projective compactifications extending the maps to the compactified curve and unchanged over \\(I\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a rational Hodge map\n\\[\nc:H^3(Z^S)\\to H^3(Z^X)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\ne\\,\\operatorname{res}_X(cz)\n=\nH^1(I,a)(\\operatorname{res}_S z).\n\\]\nThe open source and projected target are canonically their \\(H^1(R^2)\\)-terms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Resolve the closure of \\(R=E^S\\times_I E^X\\) in the fiber product of compactifications, preserving \\(R\\). This gives a smooth projective fivefold \\(\\overline R\\) with maps \\(\\pi_S,\\pi_X\\).</p><p>Fiberwise Poincaré duality represents \\(a\\) by a flat rational \\((2,2)\\)-section\n\\[\n\\alpha\\in H^0(I,R^4(R/I)_*\\mathbf Q)\n\\]\nin the \\(H^2\\otimes H^2\\) summand. The global invariant cycle theorem identifies it with a restriction from \\(H^4(\\overline R)\\). Semisimplicity splits restriction onto its image as a Hodge map, giving a rational \\((2,2)\\)-lift \\(\\widetilde\\alpha\\). Its restrictions to all fibers are \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nc(z)=(\\pi_X)_*(\\widetilde\\alpha\\cup\\pi_S^*z).\n\\]\nIt preserves degree and is Hodge. On the open it induces the relative cohomological correspondence with degree-two coefficient action \\(a\\). Since \\(R^3h_{S*}=0\\) and \\(eR^3h_{X*}=0\\), Lemma \\(13.1\\) identifies the entire relevant \\(H^3\\) with \\(H^1(R^2)\\) and proves the formula. No algebraicity of \\(\\widetilde\\alpha\\) is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Its first square pullbacks have models \\(Z\\) projective over \\(\\mathbf A_s^1\\), smooth over a nonempty \\(G\\subset O\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathscr Y\\to O\\) be a smooth projective surface family.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Its first square pullbacks have models \\(Z\\) projective over \\(\\mathbf A_s^1\\), smooth over a nonempty \\(G\\subset O\\). They can be fixed so that, for every \\(b\\in G\\), there is a smooth projective fourfold \\(M_b\\), mapping to \\(\\mathbf P_t^1\\) and \\(\\mathbf P_v^1\\), whose inverse images of\n\\[\nV_0=\\{v\\in\\mathbf A^1:b+v^2\\in G\\}\n\\]\nand \\(O\\), respectively, are the actual opens\n\\[\nZ_G\\times_GV_0,\\qquad\n\\mathscr Y\\times_OQ_b,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nQ_b=\\{u^2+v^2=(t-b)w^2\\}\\subset O\\times\\mathbf P^2.\n\\]\nThe identifications agree on the intersection, and \\(G\\) may be shrunk before choosing \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a smooth projective compactification \\(\\overline{\\mathscr Y}\\to\\mathbf P_t^1\\) unchanged over \\(O\\). Its map is flat, with geometrically integral generic fiber. Pull back by the morphism\n\\[\n\\mathbf P_u^1\\times\\mathbf A_s^1\\to\\mathbf P_t^1,\\qquad\n[U_0:U_1]\\mapsto[U_0^2+sU_1^2:U_1^2].\n\\]\nResolve by projective blowups supported over \\(t\\notin O\\). After removing finitely many \\(s\\)-values, the resulting \\(Z_G\\to G\\) is smooth. Fix this construction.</p><p>Let \\(B_b\\) be the normalization of \\(\\mathbf P_t^1\\times\\mathbf P_v^1\\) in\n\\[\n\\mathbf C(t,v)(\\sqrt{t-b-v^2}).\n\\]\nIts finite-\\(v\\) open is \\(\\mathbf P_u^1\\times\\mathbf A_v^1\\). Its open over \\(O\\) is \\(Q_b\\): the map\n\\[\n(t,[u:v:w])\\mapsto(t,[v:w])\n\\]\nis finite of degree two and has the stated function field. The conic surface is smooth, by the \\(t\\)-derivative on \\(w=1\\) and the \\(u,v\\)-derivatives at infinity.</p><p>On the finite-\\(v\\) open of \\(\\overline{\\mathscr Y}\\times_{\\mathbf P_t^1}B_b\\), pull back the fixed blowups by \\(s=b+v^2\\); finite flat base change commutes with blowup. Extend each center ideal \\(I\\) from the open immersion \\(j:U\\hookrightarrow W\\) by\n\\[\nJ=\\ker(\\mathcal O_W\\to j_*(\\mathcal O_U/I)).\n\\]\nIt is coherent on the noetherian scheme, restricts to \\(I\\), and is the unit ideal off the closed boundary over \\(t\\notin O\\). Extend the finite blowup sequence, then resolve while preserving the smooth union of the two required opens. This gives \\(M_b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are smooth projective curves \\(C_1,C_2\\) and \\( Z\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S_b^0\\times C_1\\times C_2)_{\\mathbf Q} \\) whose action induces an injection …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose abelian data and a section pencil as in Proposition \\(10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a final parameter \\(b\\) in the common geometric good open, outside the countable Hodge exceptions and comparison exceptions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are smooth projective curves \\(C_1,C_2\\) and\n\\[\nZ\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S_b^0\\times C_1\\times C_2)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nwhose action induces an injection\n\\[\nf:\\mathcal U_b\\hookrightarrow H^1(C_1,\\mathbf Q)\\otimes H^1(C_2,\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_b=T_{S_b^0},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Hg}(\\mathcal U_b)=\\operatorname{SO}(\\mathcal U_b),\n\\]\nand its quadratic form is the \\(Y\\)-form, realized as K3 cup product.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(14.1\\) gives the exact identification \\(\\varphi_b\\) algebraically. At the selected periods, Proposition \\(8\\) gives no rational \\((1,1)\\)-classes in \\(U_{X_b}\\). Proposition \\(9\\) gives a product of curves dominating \\(X_b\\) and the injection represented by\n\\[\nZ_X=(\\pi_1^{C_1}\\boxtimes\\pi_1^{C_2})\\circ{}^t\\Gamma_g\\circ e.\n\\]\nSet \\(Z=Z_X\\circ\\Theta_b\\). Its action is \\(g^*\\varphi_b\\), hence injective, and its codimension is \\(2+2-2=2\\).</p><p>Propositions \\(8\\) and \\(11\\) identify \\(\\mathcal U_b\\) with the full transcendental space and give the full special orthogonal group. The comparison to \\(X_b\\) satisfies \\(\\varphi_b^*q_X=2q_Y\\); this factor of two belongs to the curve correspondence, not to the K3 isometry.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Corollary \\(6.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a nonempty open of the fixed pencil, for all but countably many \\(b\\), there is \\( \\Theta_b\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S_b^0\\times X_b)_{\\mathbf Q} \\) inducing the specified variation identification …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix abelian-cover data and a section pencil satisfying Propositions \\(7\\), \\(8\\), and \\(11\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the \\(\\mathcal U\\)-period is nonconstant and the fibers are simple outside a countable set.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a nonempty open of the fixed pencil, for all but countably many \\(b\\), there is\n\\[\n\\Theta_b\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S_b^0\\times X_b)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\ninducing the specified variation identification\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_b\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^2(X_b)^{j=+1,\\delta=-1}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions \\(7\\) and \\(11\\) supply the variation, projector, and identification. Proposition \\(11\\) gives uniruled first square-pullback models on the K3 side. On the cover side, the generic fiber over \\(B'\\) after \\(t=s+u^2\\) is the conic\n\\[\nz^2-\\pi^*d_1u^2=\\pi^*(d_0+sd_1)\n\\]\nover \\(\\mathbf C(B')\\). A finite extension splits it, so a generically finite surface cover of \\(B'\\) gives domination by a product with \\(\\mathbf P^1\\). Thus every smooth projective threefold model is uniruled. Proposition \\(13\\) applies to each fixed pencil, with its countable exceptions chosen afterward.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The rational map \\( \\Phi:\\mathcal H\\oplus\\mathcal H\\to \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(U,W\\otimes W), \\) defined by \\( \\Phi(a\\otimes b,0)=(a\\otimes b)f,\\qquad \\Phi(0,a\\otimes b)=\\tau(a\\otimes b)f, \\) is surjective.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((U,q)\\) be an eight-dimensional polarized rational K3-type Hodge structure of signature \\((2,6)\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Hg}(U)=\\operatorname{SO}(U,q).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_1,W_2\\) be polarized effective weight-one Hodge structures.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W=C^+(U,q)\\) have its left Kuga–Satake Hodge structure.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a nonzero Hodge map \\(f:U\\to W_1\\otimes W_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(W_1,W)\n  \\otimes_{\\mathbf Q}\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(W_2,W),\n  \\]\n  and let \\(\\tau\\) be the ordinary flip of \\(W\\otimes W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rational map\n\\[\n\\Phi:\\mathcal H\\oplus\\mathcal H\\to\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(U,W\\otimes W),\n\\]\ndefined by\n\\[\n\\Phi(a\\otimes b,0)=(a\\otimes b)f,\\qquad\n\\Phi(0,a\\otimes b)=\\tau(a\\otimes b)f,\n\\]\nis surjective.</p><p>If \\(U\\subset H^2(M,\\mathbf Q)\\) for a smooth projective surface \\(M\\), \\(W_i=H^1(C_i,\\mathbf Q)\\), \\(W=H^1(A,\\mathbf Q)\\), and \\(f\\) is induced by a codimension-two algebraic correspondence from \\(M\\) to \\(C_1\\times C_2\\), then every map in the target of \\(\\Phi\\) is induced by a cycle in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CH}^2(M\\times A\\times A)_{\\mathbf Q}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nW_{i,\\mathbf C}^{\\mathrm{nt}}\n=(S_+\\otimes M_{i,+})\\oplus(S_-\\otimes M_{i,-}).\n\\]\nBy Lemma \\(15.2\\), the nonzero map \\(f_{\\mathbf C}\\) is specified by\n\\[\nt_{+-}\\in M_{1,+}\\otimes M_{2,-},\n\\qquad\nt_{-+}\\in M_{1,-}\\otimes M_{2,+},\n\\]\nat least one nonzero. Terms involving a trivial \\(\\mathfrak s\\)-constituent contain no standard summand.</p><p>If \\(t_{+-}\\ne0\\), choose functionals \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) with\n\\[\n(\\ell_1\\otimes\\ell_2)(t_{+-})\\ne0.\n\\]\nRank-one maps \\(m\\mapsto\\ell_1(m)n_+\\), \\(m\\mapsto\\ell_2(m)n_-\\), zero on the other signs, send this tensor to a nonzero scalar multiple of any decomposable \\(n_+\\otimes n_-\\). They span \\(N_+\\otimes N_-\\). The second copy in \\(\\Phi\\), followed by flip, gives the other orientation. The case \\(t_{-+}\\ne0\\) is identical. Thus \\(\\Phi_{\\mathbf C}\\) is onto, and its rational cokernel is zero.</p><p>For algebraicity, a rational Hodge map \\(a:H^1(C_i)\\to H^1(A)\\) is represented by its Poincaré-dual tensor\n\\[\n\\alpha_a\\in H^1(C_i)\\otimes H^1(A)\\subset H^2(C_i\\times A),\n\\]\nnormalized to have action \\(a\\). It has type \\((1,1)\\). The exponential sequence, after clearing denominators, gives a holomorphic line bundle, and GAGA makes it algebraic. Hence a rational divisor \\(D_a\\) has exactly that middle Künneth class.</p><p>Products \\(D_a\\boxtimes D_b\\) act by \\(a\\otimes b\\). Composing with the given surface-to-curves cycle preserves codimension two. If \\(s:A^2\\to A^2\\) interchanges factors, then\n\\[\ns^*(p_1^*u\\cup p_2^*v)\n=p_2^*u\\cup p_1^*v\n=-p_1^*v\\cup p_2^*u.\n\\]\nThus \\(-\\Gamma_s\\) induces the ordinary flip. A rational preimage under \\(\\Phi\\), realized by a finite sum of divisor products and these signed interchanges, gives exactly the target map in the full \\(H^2(A^2)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The rational Lie algebra of \\(G\\) splits as \\( \\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak s\\oplus\\mathfrak k,\\qquad \\mathfrak s\\simeq\\mathfrak{so}(U,q), \\) where \\(\\mathfrak k\\) is the kernel of the action on \\(U\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition \\(15\\), and put\n  \\[\n  G=\\operatorname{Hg}(W_1\\oplus W_2\\oplus W).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rational Lie algebra of \\(G\\) splits as\n\\[\n\\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak s\\oplus\\mathfrak k,\\qquad\n\\mathfrak s\\simeq\\mathfrak{so}(U,q),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathfrak k\\) is the kernel of the action on \\(U\\).</p><p>The nontrivial \\(\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbf C}\\)-constituents of \\(W_{i,\\mathbf C}\\) are only the two half-spin modules \\(S_+,S_-\\). On their multiplicity spaces the infinitesimal Hodge-circle action from \\(\\mathfrak k\\) is zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Polarizability gives complements to rational invariant subspaces of the weight-one direct sum, so \\(G\\) is reductive.</p><p>The standard Clifford embedding\n\\[\nj_w:U(1)\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{End}(W)\n\\]\ngives a \\(G\\)-action on \\(U\\) preserving \\(q\\). Its image contains the Hodge circle and therefore equals \\(\\operatorname{SO}(U,q)\\). Since \\(\\mathfrak{so}(U,q)\\) is absolutely simple, exactly one rational simple ideal of the derived Lie algebra maps nontrivially to it; this map is an isomorphism. The remaining ideals and center form \\(\\mathfrak k\\).</p><p>Normalize the complex infinitesimal Hodge generator \\(D\\) to have eigenvalues \\(1,-1\\) on weight one; on \\(U\\) it has eigenvalues \\(2,0,\\ldots,0,-2\\). Write \\(D=D_{\\mathfrak s}+D_{\\mathfrak k}\\). On an isotypic summand \\(R_\\lambda\\otimes M\\), the second term acts on \\(M\\), and eigenvalues add.</p><p>Use \\(D_4\\)-coordinates with roots \\(\\pm e_i\\pm e_j\\), and \\(D_{\\mathfrak s}\\) pairing as \\(2e_1\\). A dominant highest weight has\n\\[\n\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\lambda_3\\ge|\\lambda_4|,\n\\]\nwith all coordinates integral or all half-integral. The Weyl orbit gives values \\(2\\lambda_1,-2\\lambda_1\\). Translation by an eigenvalue on \\(M\\) cannot decrease this spread. Weight-one effectivity implies \\(4\\lambda_1\\le2\\). Thus only the trivial representation and half-spins of highest weights\n\\[\n\\tfrac12(e_1+e_2+e_3+e_4),\\qquad\n\\tfrac12(e_1+e_2+e_3-e_4)\n\\]\noccur.</p><p>Each half-spin has both eigenvalues \\(1,-1\\). If \\(\\mu\\) is a multiplicity-space eigenvalue, both \\(\\mu+1,\\mu-1\\) must lie in \\(\\{1,-1\\}\\), forcing \\(\\mu=0\\). Semisimplicity gives zero action.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are rational equalities \\( \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(W_i,W) =\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathfrak s}(W_i^{\\mathrm{nt}},W), \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition \\(15\\) and Lemma \\(15.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  W_i=W_i^{\\mathfrak s}\\oplus W_i^{\\mathrm{nt}},\n  \\qquad W_i^{\\mathrm{nt}}=\\mathfrak sW_i.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are rational equalities\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(W_i,W)\n=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathfrak s}(W_i^{\\mathrm{nt}},W),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(U,W\\otimes W)\n=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathfrak s}(U,W\\otimes W).\n\\]\nAfter complexification,\n\\[\nW_{\\mathbf C}=(S_+\\otimes N_+)\\oplus(S_-\\otimes N_-),\n\\qquad \\dim N_+=\\dim N_-=8,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbf C}}\n(U_{\\mathbf C},W_{\\mathbf C}\\otimes W_{\\mathbf C})\n\\simeq(N_+\\otimes N_-)\\oplus(N_-\\otimes N_+).\n\\]\nThe flip interchanges the two summands.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma \\(15.1\\), the Hodge-circle generator acts through \\(\\mathfrak s\\) on \\(W_i^{\\mathrm{nt}}\\). The same holds on \\(W\\): its Hodge group lies in the rational spin group acting by left multiplication and maps onto \\(\\operatorname{SO}(U,q)\\) with finite kernel, so \\(\\mathfrak k\\) acts trivially. A rational \\(\\mathfrak s\\)-intertwiner is therefore Hodge, and the converse follows from commutation with \\(G\\). There are no invariant summands in \\(W\\).</p><p>For \\(U_{\\mathbf C}=F\\oplus F^\\vee\\), \\(\\dim F=4\\), exterior multiplication and contraction identify\n\\[\nC^+(U)_{\\mathbf C}\\simeq\n\\operatorname{End}(\\bigwedge^{\\mathrm{even}}F)\n\\oplus\n\\operatorname{End}(\\bigwedge^{\\mathrm{odd}}F).\n\\]\nEach half-spin has dimension eight, so left multiplication gives eight copies of each.</p><p>Intertwiner spaces are kernels of rational linear maps, hence commute with scalar extension. Thus all complex multiplicity-space maps occur after complexifying the rational Hodge Hom spaces, without requiring separate rational half-spin types.</p><p>Both half-spins are self-dual. Clifford antisymmetrization, together with a normalized volume element, gives\n\\[\nS_+\\otimes S_-\\simeq U_{\\mathbf C}\\oplus\\bigwedge^3U_{\\mathbf C},\n\\]\n\\[\nS_\\pm\\otimes S_\\pm\\simeq\n\\mathbf C\\oplus\\bigwedge^2U_{\\mathbf C}\\oplus\\bigwedge^4_\\pm U_{\\mathbf C}.\n\\]\nThere is no standard summand in \\(\\bigwedge^3U_{\\mathbf C}\\): an equivariant map would send a unit vector to an invariant of its \\(\\operatorname{SO}_7\\)-stabilizer in\n\\[\n\\bigwedge^3u^\\perp\\oplus u\\wedge\\bigwedge^2u^\\perp,\n\\]\nand determinant-one sign changes kill every coefficient. Same-sign products have none because \\(-\\operatorname{id}\\) acts trivially on even exterior powers and by \\(-1\\) on \\(U\\). Thus each opposite-sign product has standard multiplicity one, giving the formula. In particular the rational Hom dimension is \\(8^2+8^2=128\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition \\(7.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\( \\Gamma_S\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S\\times A\\times A)_{\\mathbf Q} \\) whose action on \\(T_S\\) is exactly \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) in the full \\(H^2(A\\times A,\\mathbf Q)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be a construction point of Proposition \\(14\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a projective K3 surface with a rational Hodge isometry\n  \\[\n  r_T:T_S\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathcal U_b=T_{S_b^0}\n  \\]\n  for their cup-product forms.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix any full even-Clifford realization \\(H^1(A,\\mathbf Q)=C^+(T_S,q)\\), rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T_S\\), and rational polarization \\(E\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is\n\\[\n\\Gamma_S\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S\\times A\\times A)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nwhose action on \\(T_S\\) is exactly \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) in the full \\(H^2(A\\times A,\\mathbf Q)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The isometry uses the \\(Y\\)-form, realized by the auxiliary K3. The factor-two pullback to \\(X_b\\) belongs only to the curve correspondence.</p><p>Mark both full second cohomology spaces by \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3},\\mathbf Q}\\). Witt’s extension theorem extends \\(r_T\\) to\n\\[\nr:H^2(S,\\mathbf Q)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^2(S_b^0,\\mathbf Q).\n\\]\nIt carries the orthogonal complements, namely the Néron–Severi spaces, to each other; both are of type \\((1,1)\\). Thus it is a full rational Hodge isometry. Buskin’s theorem gives\n\\[\nR\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S\\times S_b^0)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\ninducing \\(r\\).</p><p>Compose with Proposition \\(14\\) to get an algebraic injection\n\\[\nT_S\\hookrightarrow H^1(C_1)\\otimes H^1(C_2).\n\\]\nAt these periods \\(\\operatorname{Hg}(T_S)=\\operatorname{SO}(T_S)\\). Proposition \\(15\\) realizes every Hodge map into the full even-Clifford tensor square, in particular the prescribed \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\). There is no extra component in other degree-two Künneth summands.</p><p>The construction choices retain the order of Proposition \\(10\\) and Lemma \\(14.1\\). The auxiliary integral Néron–Severi lattice need not equal \\(P\\).</p><p>As noted in original Remark \\(7.4\\), Buskin’s theorem requires neither integrality nor an ample chamber. In its order-one cyclic reduction, a sign and finitely many algebraic root reflections reduce to a chamber-preserving integral isometry, realized by strong Torelli. In its rational-reflection chain, transported positive rational \\((1,1)\\)-classes make all intermediate K3 surfaces projective after clearing denominators.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The set of \\(b\\in B\\) admitting a rational codimension-\\(r\\) algebraic cycle \\(Z_b\\) such that \\( L_b(\\operatorname{cl}(Z_b))=\\tau_b \\) is a countable union of closed analytic subsets.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:\\mathscr Z\\to B\\) be a smooth proper holomorphic family of pure relative dimension \\(n\\) over a connected coordinate ball, with a closed relative embedding \\(\\mathscr Z\\subset\\mathbf P^N\\times B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0\\le r\\le n\\), a rational local system \\(\\mathscr V\\), a local-system morphism\n  \\[\n  L:R^{2r}f_*\\mathbf Q\\to\\mathscr V,\n  \\]\n  and a flat section \\(\\tau\\) of \\(\\mathscr V\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set of \\(b\\in B\\) admitting a rational codimension-\\(r\\) algebraic cycle \\(Z_b\\) such that\n\\[\nL_b(\\operatorname{cl}(Z_b))=\\tau_b\n\\]\nis a countable union of closed analytic subsets. Each is the image of a proper analytic parameter space carrying a fixed rational combination of universal flat subschemes, every point of which supplies a successful cycle.</p><p>If the successful set contains a nonempty open, it is all of \\(B\\). The same holds if it escapes every countable union of proper closed analytic subsets.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(R\\) be the Hilbert polynomial of the embedded fibers. For each Hilbert polynomial \\(Q\\), use the projective Hilbert scheme \\(K_Q=\\operatorname{Hilb}^Q(\\mathbf P^N)\\) and the incidence space\n\\[\nH_Q=\\{(b,z)\\in B\\times K_Q^{\\mathrm{an}}:U_z\\subset\\mathscr Z_b\\}.\n\\]\nIt is closed analytic: bounded regularity for fixed \\(R,Q\\) gives a degree \\(k\\) in which the ambient ideal is relatively generated and relevant pushforwards are vector bundles with base change. Containment is the vanishing of the restriction map of these bundles. Compactness of \\(K_Q^{\\mathrm{an}}\\) makes \\(H_Q\\to B\\) proper. The universal subscheme is flat over \\(H_Q\\).</p><p>For \\(\\deg Q=n-r\\), its fundamental cycle class in ambient \\(H^{2r}\\) is locally constant, including at singular and nonreduced parameters. To justify this, the degree-\\(k\\) ideal construction gives a holomorphic classifying map to the smooth-fiber locus of the algebraic Hilbert scheme \\(K_R\\). The analytic incidence pair is the pullback of the algebraic universal relative Hilbert pair.</p><p>Its universal structure sheaf \\(\\mathcal F\\) is flat over the parameter space and perfect on the smooth-relative ambient space. Successive locally free surjections have parameter-flat kernels; after at most \\(n\\) syzygies, fiberwise regularity and the local flatness criterion make the kernel locally free. The resulting bounded vector-bundle resolution restricts to ordinary fiber resolutions.</p><p>The leading Chern-character identity is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}_r(\\mathcal F)|_{\\mathscr Z_b}\n=\n\\operatorname{cl}([U_z]_{n-r}).\n\\]\nThis is GRR and dévissage with generic lengths, followed by comparison of algebraic and topological Chern classes. Removing codimension-at-least-\\(r+1\\) subsets reduces to smooth generic supports; such subsets contribute nothing in degree \\(2r\\). Lower-dimensional embedded components therefore do not matter.</p><p>A class on the total pulled-back smooth proper family restricts flatly to fibers, by local differentiable trivialization and homotopy along parameter paths. Analytic spaces are locally path connected, and nilpotents do not affect topology.</p><p>Take all finite lists of polynomials and rational coefficients:\n\\[\nH_{\\mathbf Q}=H_{Q_1}\\times_B\\cdots\\times_BH_{Q_s},\n\\qquad\nZ_{\\mathbf c,z}=\\sum_i c_i[U_{i,z}]_{n-r},\n\\]\nincluding the empty list. On each connected component its ambient class, and hence success, is constant, since \\(B\\) is simply connected. There are countably many lists and components. Each successful component is closed analytic and proper over \\(B\\). Remmert’s theorem makes its image closed analytic.</p><p>Every rational cycle occurs, and every point of a retained image has an actual universal fiber combination. Chow’s theorem makes its projective-fiber supports algebraic. This proves the exact description.</p><p>Proper closed analytic subsets of a connected ball are nowhere dense. Baire’s theorem and the identity principle prove the open-set conclusion. If all images were proper, their own countable union would contradict the escape hypothesis.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The group \\(O(V)(\\mathbf Q)\\) is dense in \\(O(V_{\\mathbf R})\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((V,q)\\) be a rational quadratic space of signature \\((2,n)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The group \\(O(V)(\\mathbf Q)\\) is dense in \\(O(V_{\\mathbf R})\\). The rational orbit of every point of its type-IV period domain meets every nonempty open subset.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cartan–Dieudonné writes a real orthogonal transformation as a finite product of reflections in nonisotropic real vectors. The reflection\n\\[\nr_v(x)=x-\\frac{2q(x,v)}{q(v,v)}v\n\\]\ndepends continuously on \\(v\\) on the nonisotropic open and is rational for rational \\(v\\). Approximate each vector rationally. The full real orthogonal group is transitive on oriented positive two-planes and interchanges the two components, so density gives the orbit assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Proposition \\(8.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a full even-Clifford realization \\( W_U=C^+(U_P,q)=H^1(A_U,\\mathbf Q), \\) a rational polarization \\(E_0\\), a rational nonisotropic \\(w_0\\in U_P\\), and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) have an ample primitive \\(P\\)-polarization, and put\n  \\[\n  U_P=P_{\\mathbf Q}^{\\perp}\\subset H^2(S,\\mathbf Q).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a full even-Clifford realization\n\\[\nW_U=C^+(U_P,q)=H^1(A_U,\\mathbf Q),\n\\]\na rational polarization \\(E_0\\), a rational nonisotropic \\(w_0\\in U_P\\), and\n\\[\n\\Gamma_U\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S\\times A_U^2)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\nwhose full degree-two action on every \\(x\\in U_P\\) is exactly \\(\\iota_{w_0,E_0}(x)\\).</p><p>The source is the whole fixed rank-eight space, including its rational \\((1,1)\\)-classes at Picard jumps.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a marking and an integral ample \\(h\\in P\\). Local Torelli gives a six-dimensional marked deformation ball \\(B\\) through \\(S\\), keeping \\(P\\) algebraic and identified with an open of the period domain of \\(U_P\\). Shrink so that \\(h\\) is relatively ample and a power embeds the K3 family projectively. Only \\(P\\), not the whole target Picard group, is fixed.</p><p>Fix rational nonisotropic \\(w_0\\in U_P\\). One rational alternating form polarizes the Clifford structures throughout the period component. With reversal \\(x\\mapsto x^*\\), put\n\\[\nB_C(x,y)=\\operatorname{scal}(x^*y),\n\\qquad\nE_0(x,y)=B_C(x,y\\beta),\n\\]\nup to sign, where \\(\\beta\\) is the oriented product of two orthogonal positive rational vectors. Reversal sends \\(\\beta\\) to \\(-\\beta\\), proving alternation, and left spin multiplication preserves the form because \\(g^*g=1\\). At the chosen plane \\(\\beta=cJ\\), \\(c&gt;0\\); for an even orthogonal monomial containing \\(a\\) positive generators,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sign}B_C(x,x)=(-1)^a,\\qquad\nJx\\beta=(-1)^{a+1}cx.\n\\]\nThus \\(E_0(x,Jx)\\) is definite after one sign choice. Spin transitivity gives positivity throughout.</p><p>The \\((1,0)\\)-space is \\(\\ker L_\\sigma\\) and varies holomorphically. A rational symplectic basis and a self-dual integral lattice give, after shrinking, a polarized holomorphic abelian family \\(\\mathscr A\\to B\\), relatively projective by the usual Siegel-space torus and theta construction. The exact tensor\n\\[\n\\tau=\\iota_{w_0,E_0}:U_P\\to W_U\\otimes W_U\n\\]\nis flat and Hodge at every period.</p><p>Choose a regular geometric datum in the simultaneous open of Propositions \\(8\\) and \\(9\\). Propositions \\(1\\) and \\(7\\) give a rational isometry \\(\\theta:\\mathcal U\\simeq U_P\\), using the \\(Y\\)-form. Proposition \\(18\\) gives \\(g\\in O(U_P)(\\mathbf Q)\\) moving its period into \\(B\\). Fix \\(g\\theta\\), and shrink the original normalized geometric chart so its period map followed by \\(g\\theta\\) is a submersion into \\(B\\). This changes only rational period coordinates, not the algebraic section pencils.</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(17\\) to\n\\[\n\\mathscr Z=\\mathscr S\\times_B\\mathscr A^2\n\\]\nof relative dimension \\(130\\), with \\(r=2\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathscr V=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf Q}(U_P,R^2(\\mathscr A^2/B)_*\\mathbf Q),\n\\qquad\nL_b(\\gamma)(x)=(p_{AA})_*(\\gamma\\cup p_S^*x).\n\\]\nSuccess means equality to \\(\\tau\\) on all of \\(U_P\\) in the full target. Let \\(Y_i\\) be its countably many proper-parameter analytic images.</p><p>Suppose all \\(Y_i\\) are proper. Pull them back to the regular geometric chart by \\(g\\theta\\), and add the extra-Picard hyperplanes of \\(B\\) and the proper marked Hodge-tensor conditions. These loci are proper: the period map is open, and an extra integral class has nonzero \\(U_P\\)-projection because \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{K3}}\\cap P_{\\mathbf Q}=P\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(10\\) first selects abelian data, then an admissible pencil with good leading section. Fix that pencil, its first models, and relative correspondence; then add the countable exceptions of Lemma \\(14.1\\). Finally choose a parameter outside all exceptions and prescribed loci. Proposition \\(14\\) supplies the curve source and full special orthogonal group.</p><p>The target fiber at the moved period has \\(\\operatorname{NS}=P\\), so its full \\(U_P\\) is transcendental. The exact rational period isometry \\(g\\theta\\) identifies its transcendental space with that of the auxiliary K3. Proposition \\(16\\) gives a cycle realizing the fixed \\(\\tau\\). This target lies in a successful image, contradicting its choice.</p><p>Hence one successful image is all of \\(B\\), and its fiber over the original target supplies \\(\\Gamma_U\\). The whole rank-eight source and full target action remained fixed; neither a global moduli cycle nor specialization of the curve presentation is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The exact standard tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) has a rational codimension-two algebraic realization on \\(S\\times A\\times A\\), including when \\(S\\) is a Picard-jump fiber.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be an ample \\(P\\)-polarized K3 surface.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T_S\\), a rational polarization \\(E\\) of \\(C^+(T_S,q)\\), and a specified transported even-Clifford realization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact standard tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) has a rational codimension-two algebraic realization on \\(S\\times A\\times A\\), including when \\(S\\) is a Picard-jump fiber.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(U=U_P\\) and \\(M=U\\cap\\operatorname{NS}(S)_{\\mathbf Q}\\). Since \\(P\\) contains an ample class, Hodge index gives\n\\[\nU=T_S\\perp M\n\\]\nwith \\(M\\) negative definite. Projection to \\(T_S\\) is algebraic by divisor bases, inverse intersection matrix, and diagonal.</p><p>Proposition \\(19\\) gives one exact \\(\\iota_{w_0,E_0}\\) on all of \\(U\\). For the desired \\(w\\), put\n\\[\nr=w_0^{-1}w\\in C^+(U,q).\n\\]\nRight multiplication \\(R_r\\) is an invertible rational Hodge map, and\n\\[\nj_w=R_rj_{w_0},\\qquad\n\\iota_{w,E_0}=(R_r\\otimes\\operatorname{id})\\iota_{w_0,E_0}.\n\\]</p><p>PBW gives a natural injection\n\\[\ni:C^+(T_S,q)\\hookrightarrow C^+(U,q).\n\\]\nIt is Hodge because the left period bivector lies in the smaller algebra. Polarizability gives a Hodge retraction \\(p\\). For \\(v,w\\in T_S\\), \\(j_w(v)\\) preserves the smaller algebra and restricts to \\(a\\mapsto vaw\\). Lemma \\(20.1\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n\\left(pR_r\\otimes(E^\\flat)^{-1}i^\\vee(-1)E_0^\\flat\\right)\n\\iota_{w_0,E_0}\n=\n\\iota_{w,E}^{T_S}.\n\\]\nThe order is retained: \\(R_r\\) itself need not preserve the smaller algebra, but \\(j_w\\) does.</p><p>Both slot maps are algebraic on abelian \\(H^1\\). After clearing denominators, their product homomorphism and normalized transpose graph realize their tensor product. Its codimension is \\(\\dim A_U^2\\), so postcomposition preserves codimension two. Source projection and degree-one target projectors isolate exactly the required full degree-two tensor. The same argument transports to any stipulated isogenous realization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For tensor forms, \\( (p\\otimes b)\\iota_{j,E_U}=\\iota_{pji,E_T}. \\) If these weight-one structures are abelian \\(H^1\\)-spaces, both tensor-slot maps are induced by rational algebraic correspondences.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(i:W_T\\hookrightarrow W_U\\) be an inclusion of polarizable rational weight-one Hodge structures, and \\(p:W_U\\to W_T\\) a Hodge retraction.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_U,E_T\\) be rational polarizations.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) have weight zero, and \\(j:H\\to\\operatorname{End}(W_U)\\) be Hodge.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  b=(E_T^\\flat)^{-1}i^\\vee(-1)E_U^\\flat:W_U\\to W_T.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For tensor forms,\n\\[\n(p\\otimes b)\\iota_{j,E_U}=\\iota_{pji,E_T}.\n\\]\nIf these weight-one structures are abelian \\(H^1\\)-spaces, both tensor-slot maps are induced by rational algebraic correspondences.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the ordering \\(\\operatorname{End}(W_U)=W_U\\otimes W_U^\\vee\\), tensor conversion applies \\(\\operatorname{id}\\otimes(E_U^\\flat)^{-1}\\) after the common twist. The left side becomes\n\\[\n\\bigl(p\\otimes(E_T^\\flat)^{-1}i^\\vee(-1)\\bigr)j,\n\\]\nwhich is the tensor conversion of \\(pji\\).</p><p>For a rational Hodge map \\(f:H^1(B,\\mathbf Q)\\to H^1(A,\\mathbf Q)\\), the dual map on homology commutes with complex structures. For some positive integer \\(n\\), \\(nf^\\vee\\) is integral and gives a holomorphic homomorphism \\(\\phi:A\\to B\\). GAGA makes it algebraic, and \\(f=\\phi^*/n\\). Thus\n\\[\n[{}^t\\Gamma_\\phi]/n\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{\\dim B}(B\\times A)_{\\mathbf Q}\n\\]\ninduces \\(f\\), without a dimension or isogeny assumption.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The exact tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) is induced by a rational codimension-two algebraic correspondence.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a projective K3 surface admitting a rational isometric embedding\n  \\[\n  (T_S,q)\\hookrightarrow V_P.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix any rational nonisotropic \\(w\\in T_S\\), rational polarization \\(E\\), and stipulated transported isogenous full even-Clifford model.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact tensor \\(\\iota_{w,E}\\) is induced by a rational codimension-two algebraic correspondence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(R,\\Phi\\) by Lemma \\(21.1\\), and let \\(\\phi=\\Phi|_{T_S}\\). The isometry induces a unital Clifford algebra isomorphism\n\\[\nc_\\phi:C^+(T_S,q)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}C^+(T_R,q),\n\\qquad\nc_\\phi(vaw)=\\phi(v)c_\\phi(a)\\phi(w).\n\\]\nIt is Hodge because it transports the oriented period bivector defining the left complex structure.</p><p>Proposition \\(20\\) gives the exact tensor on \\(T_R\\) with right factor \\(\\phi(w)\\) and polarization transported from \\(E\\). Precompose with the algebraic correspondence for \\(\\Phi\\), and transport both abelian slots by \\(c_\\phi^{-1}\\). Lemma \\(20.1\\) algebraizes these rational Hodge maps on \\(H^1\\).</p><p>The Clifford identity proves equality with the specified tensor, not merely an abstract embedding. Source and degree-one target projectors isolate its full action. The correspondences preserve degree on intermediate factors, so the resulting codimension is two. The stipulated isogenous model is handled by the same transport.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an ample \\(P\\)-polarized K3 surface \\(R\\) and a full rational Hodge isometry \\( \\Phi:H^2(S,\\mathbf Q)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^2(R,\\mathbf Q), \\qquad \\Phi(T_S)=T_R. \\) Both directions are induced by algebraic …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) and an isometric embedding \\(\\iota:T_S\\hookrightarrow V_P\\) be as in Proposition \\(21\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an ample \\(P\\)-polarized K3 surface \\(R\\) and a full rational Hodge isometry\n\\[\n\\Phi:H^2(S,\\mathbf Q)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^2(R,\\mathbf Q),\n\\qquad\n\\Phi(T_S)=T_R.\n\\]\nBoth directions are induced by algebraic correspondences.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition \\(1\\) and an exact-\\(P\\) surface. Choose integral ample \\(h\\in P\\) and a small marked \\(P\\)-polarized period patch \\(\\Omega\\), shrinking so \\(h\\) remains ample. It is an actual open in the domain of \\(V_P\\), including Picard-jump fibers.</p><p>The line \\(z=\\iota(H^{2,0}(S))\\) lies in this domain. Proposition \\(18\\) gives \\(g\\in O(V_P)(\\mathbf Q)\\) with \\(gz\\in\\Omega\\). Take the fiber \\(R\\) of exactly that period. The isometry \\(g\\iota:T_S\\to H^2(R,\\mathbf Q)\\) takes holomorphic line to holomorphic line. Witt’s extension theorem extends it to a full rational K3-form isometry \\(\\Phi\\).</p><p>It preserves the holomorphic line, its conjugate by rationality, and their orthogonal \\((1,1)\\)-space. Thus \\(\\Phi\\) is Hodge. Lefschetz’s \\((1,1)\\)-theorem gives\n\\[\n\\Phi(\\operatorname{NS}(S)_{\\mathbf Q})=\\operatorname{NS}(R)_{\\mathbf Q},\n\\]\nhence \\(\\Phi(T_S)=T_R\\). Buskin’s theorem algebraizes \\(\\Phi\\) and its inverse. Density was used only to enter the patch; the comparison uses the exact realized period.</p>"}], "edges": [[5, 4, "part"], [6, 4, "part"], [7, 4, "part"], [9, 8, "part"], [11, 10, "part"], [12, 10, "part"], [13, 10, "part"], [15, 14, "part"], [18, 17, "part"], [19, 17, "part"], [21, 20, "part"], [22, 20, "part"], [24, 23, "part"], [25, 23, "part"], [26, 23, "part"], [27, 23, "part"], [30, 29, "part"], [31, 29, "part"], [32, 29, "part"], [33, 29, "part"], [34, 29, "part"], [37, 36, "part"], [38, 36, "part"], [39, 36, "part"], [41, 40, "part"], [43, 42, "part"], [44, 42, "part"], [50, 49, "part"], [52, 51, "part"]]});
